var e=[{id:`assign`,term:`=`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`=`]},short:`Puts the value on the right into the name on the left.`,simple:`Like writing a new number on a labelled box: whatever was inside before is gone.`,deep:`Assignment, not a question: comparing is <code>==</code>. In a declaration such as <code>uint8_t r = 0;</code> the <code>=</code> gives the variable its first value (initialisation).`,example:`lastHeartbeat = millis();`},{id:`arithmetic`,term:`+ - /`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`+`,`-`,`/`]},short:`Add, subtract and divide, like on a calculator.`,simple:`School maths, with one surprise: dividing two whole numbers throws away the remainder, so <code>7 / 2</code> is <code>3</code>.`,deep:`Integer division truncates towards zero; write <code>7 / 2.0f</code> to get <code>3.5</code>. Small types such as <code>uint8_t</code> are promoted to <code>int</code> before the maths, so <code>pos * 3</code> cannot overflow until it is stored back.`,example:`r = 255 - pos * 3;`},{id:`modulo`,term:`%`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`%`]},short:`The remainder after dividing: <code>7 % 3</code> is <code>1</code>.`,simple:`Like a clock face: count past 12 and you start again from 1. That makes <code>%</code> perfect for things that go round and round.`,deep:`Works on whole numbers only (use <code>fmodf</code> for floats). With a negative left side the result is negative too, so <code>-1 % 6</code> is <code>-1</code>, not <code>5</code>.`,example:`(now / kBlinkMs) % 2 ? 40 : 0`},{id:`compound-assign`,term:`+= -= *= /= %=`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`+=`,`-=`,`*=`,`/=`,`%=`]},short:`Shorthand that updates a variable using its own value: <code>x += 5</code> means <code>x = x + 5</code>.`,simple:`Like topping up a jar: you don't empty it and refill, you just add a bit more.`,deep:`The left side is evaluated once, which matters when it is something like <code>buf_[i++]</code>. The result is converted back to the variable's type, so a <code>uint8_t</code> quietly wraps past 255.`,example:`pos -= 85;`},{id:`increment`,term:`++ --`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`++`,`--`]},short:`Adds one to (<code>++</code>) or takes one from (<code>--</code>) a variable.`,simple:`A tally counter you click: one click, one more.`,deep:`<code>i++</code> gives back the old value then adds one; <code>++i</code> adds first. In a <code>for</code> loop on its own they behave the same. An unsigned <code>uint8_t hue</code> wraps from 255 to 0, which the rainbow in lesson 01 relies on.`,example:`wheel(hue++, r, g, b);`},{id:`compare`,term:`== != < > <= >=`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`==`,`!=`,`<`,`>`,`<=`,`>=`]},short:`Compare two values and answer <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>; after a type name, &lt; &gt; hold a type instead.`,simple:`Questions with a yes/no answer: is it equal? is it smaller? Note the double <code>==</code>: a single <code>=</code> would change the value instead of asking.`,deep:`Comparing signed with unsigned converts the signed side to unsigned, so <code>-1 &lt; 1u</code> is false; the compiler warns about it. In <code>std::vector&lt;Card&gt;</code> or <code>static_cast&lt;int&gt;</code> the angle brackets are template brackets, not comparisons.`,example:`if (millis() - lastHeartbeat >= kHeartbeatMs) {`},{id:`logic`,term:`&& || !`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`&&`,`||`,`!`]},short:`Combine yes/no answers: <code>&amp;&amp;</code> means and, <code>||</code> means or, <code>!</code> means not.`,simple:`"If it's raining <b>and</b> I'm outside, open the umbrella." <code>!</code> flips an answer: not raining.`,deep:`Short-circuit: <code>a &amp;&amp; b</code> never looks at <code>b</code> when <code>a</code> is false, which is why <code>queue_ &amp;&amp; xQueueReceive(queue_, ...)</code> is safe with a null queue. After a type (<code>T &amp;&amp;</code>) the double ampersand means a movable reference instead.`,example:`return queue_ && xQueueReceive(queue_, &w, 0) == pdTRUE;`},{id:`ternary`,term:`? :`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`?`]},short:`A one-line if/else that picks a value: <code>test ? this : that</code>.`,simple:`"Is the HUD on? Then say 'on', otherwise say 'off'." A question with two possible answers.`,deep:`Both branches must be convertible to one common type. Only the chosen branch is evaluated, so it is safe to guard a division or a pointer with it.`,example:`log_i("hud %s", state.hud ? "on" : "off");`},{id:`shift`,term:`<< >>`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`<<`,`>>`,`<<=`,`>>=`]},short:`Slide the bits of a number left or right; one step left doubles it, one step right halves it.`,simple:`Like moving a number's digits along a row of light switches: everything slides over and a new 0 comes in at the end.`,deep:`Used to pack and unpack bytes: <code>(r &amp; 0xF8) &lt;&lt; 8</code> puts red into the top 5 bits of an RGB565 colour. Shifting by the type's width or more is undefined behaviour.`,example:`(mac >> (8 * i)) & 0xFF`},{id:`bit-ops`,term:`| ^ ~ |= &= ^=`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`|`,`^`,`~`,`|=`,`&=`,`^=`]},short:`Work on the single bits of a number: <code>|</code> sets bits, <code>^</code> flips them, <code>~</code> inverts them all.`,simple:`Think of a number as a row of 8, 16 or 32 light switches. These operators flip chosen switches without touching the others.`,deep:`<code>flags |= bit</code> turns a bit on, <code>flags &amp;= ~bit</code> turns it off, <code>flags ^= bit</code> toggles it. Register drivers like our MFRC522 live on these. ArduinoJson reuses <code>|</code> for a default value: <code>in["type"] | ""</code>.`,example:`NIMBLE_PROPERTY::READ | NIMBLE_PROPERTY::NOTIFY`},{id:`scope`,term:`::`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`::`]},short:`Reaches inside a namespace, class or enum to find a name: <code>std::min</code> is the <code>min</code> that lives in <code>std</code>.`,simple:`Like an address: <i>London::Baker Street</i>. Two streets can share a name as long as they are in different towns.`,deep:`The scope resolution operator. <code>Button::Event::Click</code> walks two levels; a leading <code>::</code> (as in <code>::ble::activity()</code>) starts from the global namespace, so a local name can never shadow it.`,example:`return Event::LongPress;`},{id:`arrow`,term:`->`,topic:`basics`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`->`]},short:`Uses a member of the thing a pointer points to: <code>p->x</code> is the same as <code>(*p).x</code>.`,simple:`A dot (<code>.</code>) talks to an object you hold; the arrow talks to an object you only have the address of.`,deep:`Member access through a pointer. Web handlers get <code>AsyncWebServerRequest *req</code>, so it is <code>req->send(...)</code>. Following a <code>nullptr</code> crashes the ESP32 with a LoadProhibited or StoreProhibited panic, so pointers are checked first.`,example:`self->edges_ = self->edges_ + 1;`},{id:`maths`,term:`sinf() cosf() fabsf()...`,topic:`basics`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`sinf`,`cosf`,`fmodf`,`lroundf`,`fabsf`,`sqrtf`,`powf`,`log10f`,`std::abs`,`abs`,`isnan`]},short:`Maths helpers from the C library: sine, cosine, square root, rounding, absolute value and friends.`,simple:`The scientific buttons on a calculator, ready to use in code. Angles are in radians: a full turn is about 6.283.`,deep:`The <code>f</code> versions (<code>sinf</code>, <code>sqrtf</code>) work on <code>float</code>, which the ESP32-S3's hardware FPU handles quickly; the <code>double</code> versions run in software and are several times slower. <code>lroundf</code> rounds to the nearest whole number.`,example:`spawn(x, y, cosf(angle) * speed, sinf(angle) * speed, life, hue, false);`},{id:`snprintf`,term:`snprintf()`,topic:`basics`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`snprintf`,`vsnprintf`,`sprintf`]},short:`Builds a piece of text from a pattern and values, into a box of fixed size.`,simple:`A fill-in-the-blanks form: <code>"%d/%d"</code> has two blanks, and the numbers you pass fill them in order.`,deep:`<code>snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), fmt, ...)</code> never writes past the buffer and always ends the text with a <code>'\\0'</code>; too-long text is cut off. Same <code>%</code> codes as <code>Serial.printf</code>: <code>%d</code> int, <code>%u</code> unsigned, <code>%lu</code> unsigned long, <code>%.1f</code> float, <code>%s</code> text, <code>%02X</code> hex.`,example:`snprintf(text, sizeof(text), "%d/%d  %s", n, kSceneCount, name);`},{id:`void`,term:`void`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`void`]},short:`Means "nothing": a <code>void</code> function does its job but hands no value back.`,simple:`Like a doorbell: you press it and something happens, but it doesn't give you anything to hold.`,deep:`As a return type it means no result. <code>void *</code> is a pointer to memory of unknown type, as in the <code>void *arg</code> that FreeRTOS tasks and interrupt handlers receive; it must be cast back before use.`,example:`void setup() {`},{id:`bool`,term:`bool`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`bool`,`boolean`]},short:`A yes/no value: either <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.`,simple:`A light switch: on or off, nothing in between.`,deep:`Takes one byte. Numbers and pointers convert to <code>bool</code> automatically (zero and <code>nullptr</code> are false), which is why <code>if (buf_)</code> works. Arduino's <code>boolean</code> is the same type.`,example:`bool pressed_ = false;`},{id:`true-false`,term:`true / false`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`true`,`false`]},short:`The two values a <code>bool</code> can hold.`,simple:`Yes and no, written the way the compiler understands.`,deep:`Keywords of type <code>bool</code>; converted to numbers they become 1 and 0. FreeRTOS uses its own <code>pdTRUE</code>/<code>pdFALSE</code> for the same idea.`,example:`longFired_ = true;`},{id:`int`,term:`int`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`int`]},short:`A whole number that can be negative, the everyday default for counting.`,simple:`A box for whole numbers like -3, 0 or 42. No fractions allowed.`,deep:`On the ESP32 an <code>int</code> is 32 bits: about -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion. Its size differs between chips (16 bits on an Arduino Uno), which is why hardware code often prefers <code>int32_t</code> and friends that say their size.`,example:`for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++)`},{id:`long`,term:`long`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`long`]},short:`A whole number type that is at least as big as <code>int</code>; on the ESP32 both are 32 bits.`,simple:`Another box for whole numbers. On this chip it holds exactly what an <code>int</code> holds.`,deep:`<code>long</code> is 32 bits on the Xtensa ESP32 and <code>long long</code> is 64. <code>millis()</code> returns <code>unsigned long</code>, so printing it needs <code>%lu</code>; the code often casts with <code>static_cast&lt;unsigned long&gt;</code> to make that match exact.`,example:`const long n = line.substring(6).toInt();`},{id:`unsigned`,term:`unsigned`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`unsigned`,`signed`]},short:`A whole number that can't go below zero, so it reaches twice as high.`,simple:`Like a car's odometer: it only counts up from 0, and past its last digit it rolls back round to 0.`,deep:`Unsigned maths wraps around instead of overflowing: <code>0u - 1</code> is the biggest value. That wrap is exactly why <code>now - last >= interval</code> keeps working when <code>millis()</code> rolls over. <code>unsigned(x)</code> is a quick cast, handy for <code>%u</code> in printf.`,example:`Serial.printf("%u KB\\n", unsigned(bytes / 1024));`},{id:`float`,term:`float`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`float`]},short:`A number with a decimal point, like <code>3.14</code> or <code>-0.5</code>.`,simple:`A ruler with fine marks: good for measurements, but tiny rounding errors creep in, so never test two floats for exact equality.`,deep:`32 bits, about 7 significant digits. The ESP32-S3 has a single-precision FPU, so <code>float</code> maths is fast; write constants as <code>0.5f</code> so the compiler doesn't switch to slow <code>double</code>.`,example:`const float level = std::clamp((temp - 20) / 60, 0.0f, 1.0f);`},{id:`double`,term:`double`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`double`]},short:`A decimal number with about twice the precision of <code>float</code>.`,simple:`The same ruler as <code>float</code>, with even finer marks, but slower to read on this chip.`,deep:`64 bits, about 15 significant digits. The ESP32-S3 FPU only does single precision, so <code>double</code> is done in software: fine for an occasional calculation, too slow for every pixel or audio sample.`,example:`const double t = i / rate;`},{id:`char`,term:`char`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`char`]},short:`One character of text, stored as a small number; <code>const char *</code> is a whole piece of text.`,simple:`A single letter tile from a word game. Line tiles up, end with an invisible stop tile <code>'\\0'</code>, and you have a word.`,deep:`One byte. A C string is an array of <code>char</code> ending in <code>'\\0'</code>; <code>char text[32]</code> holds up to 31 letters. <code>"hello"</code> is a <code>const char *</code> to text kept in flash, while <code>'h'</code> (single quotes) is one <code>char</code>.`,example:`char text[48];`},{id:`size-t`,term:`size_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`size_t`]},short:`An unsigned whole number made for sizes and counts of things in memory.`,simple:`The number on a tape measure for memory: how many bytes, how many items. It can never be negative.`,deep:`32 bits on the ESP32, the type <code>sizeof</code> gives and <code>malloc</code> takes. Print it with <code>%u</code> after a cast, or <code>%zu</code>.`,example:`const size_t bytes = bytesUsed();`},{id:`uint8-t`,term:`uint8_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`uint8_t`,`byte`]},short:`A whole number from 0 to 255: exactly one byte.`,simple:`The smallest box: one byte, eight on/off switches. Perfect for a colour channel, a pin number or a brightness.`,deep:`<code>u</code> = unsigned, <code>int</code>, <code>8</code> bits, <code>_t</code> = a type. Defined in <code>&lt;stdint.h&gt;</code> so its size is the same on every chip. Arduino's <code>byte</code> is the same type. Past 255 it wraps to 0.`,example:`constexpr uint8_t kBrightness = 40;`},{id:`uint16-t`,term:`uint16_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`uint16_t`]},short:`A whole number from 0 to 65 535: two bytes.`,simple:`A box twice the size of a byte. Every screen colour in these labs fits in one.`,deep:`Unsigned, 16 bits. The ST7789 speaks RGB565: 5 bits red, 6 green, 5 blue packed into one <code>uint16_t</code>. Also used for BLE handles and 16-bit UUIDs.`,example:`constexpr uint16_t ink = rgb(232, 238, 246);`},{id:`uint32-t`,term:`uint32_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`uint32_t`]},short:`A whole number from 0 to about 4.29 billion: four bytes.`,simple:`A big box for big counts, like milliseconds since the board started.`,deep:`Unsigned, 32 bits: the ESP32's natural word size, so reads and writes of it are single instructions. Times from <code>millis()</code> live in it and wrap after about 49.7 days.`,example:`const uint32_t now = millis();`},{id:`uint64-t`,term:`uint64_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`uint64_t`]},short:`A huge whole number, 0 to about 18 quintillion: eight bytes.`,simple:`A box so big it never fills up in practice. The chip's 48-bit MAC address fits easily.`,deep:`Unsigned, 64 bits. On a 32-bit CPU each operation takes a few instructions, and reading one shared between an interrupt and the main code is not a single step, so the labs keep shared timestamps at 32 bits.`,example:`const uint64_t mac = ESP.getEfuseMac();`},{id:`int8-t`,term:`int8_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`int8_t`]},short:`A small whole number from -128 to 127: one byte that can be negative.`,simple:`A one-byte box with room for a minus sign. Good for things like Wi-Fi signal strength in dBm.`,deep:`Signed, 8 bits, two's complement. BLE transmit power and RSSI values are reported as <code>int8_t</code>.`,example:`int8_t txPower = 9;`},{id:`int16-t`,term:`int16_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`int16_t`]},short:`A whole number from -32 768 to 32 767: two bytes that can be negative.`,simple:`A two-byte box with a minus sign. Screen coordinates use it, so shapes can start just off the edge.`,deep:`Signed, 16 bits. Adafruit GFX takes <code>int16_t</code> for x, y, width and height, and 16-bit audio samples from the microphone are <code>int16_t</code> too.`,example:`static constexpr int16_t kW = 320, kH = 170;`},{id:`int32-t`,term:`int32_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`int32_t`]},short:`A whole number from about -2.1 billion to +2.1 billion: four bytes that can be negative.`,simple:`The same box as <code>int</code> on this chip, but its name promises the size on every chip.`,deep:`Signed, 32 bits. The INMP441 microphone sends 24-bit samples inside 32-bit I2S slots, which are read as <code>int32_t</code> and then shifted down.`,example:`const int32_t left = max<int32_t>(0, int32_t(s.modeUntil - now));`},{id:`int64-t`,term:`int64_t`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`int64_t`]},short:`A huge whole number that can be negative: eight bytes.`,simple:`A box that never runs out for clocks counting microseconds.`,deep:`Signed, 64 bits. <code>esp_timer_get_time()</code> returns <code>int64_t</code> microseconds since boot, which would take about 292 000 years to overflow.`,example:`const int64_t t0 = esp_timer_get_time();`},{id:`string-class`,term:`String`,topic:`types`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`String`]},short:`Arduino's text type: a piece of text that can grow, shrink and be searched.`,simple:`A stretchy notebook page for words: add to it, cut bits out, look for a word, without counting letters yourself.`,deep:`Keeps its letters on the heap (short ones inline), so repeated <code>+</code> in a busy loop churns memory. The labs use <code>String</code> for serial commands and web replies and fixed <code>char</code> arrays where speed or interrupts matter. <code>String(42)</code> turns a number into text.`,example:`String lower = line;`},{id:`auto`,term:`auto`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`auto`]},short:`Lets the compiler work out the type from the value you give it.`,simple:`Like saying "put it in a box of the right size" instead of naming the box.`,deep:`The type is still fixed at compile time, not dynamic. <code>auto *self = static_cast&lt;Button *&gt;(arg);</code> avoids writing <code>Button</code> twice; <code>auto</code> is also how lambdas are stored, since their type has no name.`,example:`auto *self = static_cast<Button *>(arg);`},{id:`nullptr`,term:`nullptr`,topic:`types`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`nullptr`,`NULL`]},short:`A pointer that points at nothing.`,simple:`An empty address label: there is no house to visit, so check before you go.`,deep:`The type-safe null pointer constant (C++11), replacing <code>NULL</code> and <code>0</code>. Allocation functions such as <code>heap_caps_malloc</code> return it on failure, and many ESP-IDF calls take it for "I don't need this".`,example:`if (buf_ == nullptr) return false;`},{id:`if`,term:`if`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`if`]},short:`Runs the next block only when the test in brackets is true.`,simple:`"If it's raining, take an umbrella." When it isn't raining, the umbrella step is skipped.`,deep:`The condition is converted to <code>bool</code>, so numbers and pointers work directly. A declaration in the test (<code>if (DeserializationError e = deserializeJson(...))</code>) keeps the variable inside the if.`,example:`if (ESP.getPsramSize() == 0) {`},{id:`else`,term:`else`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`else`]},short:`The other path: runs when the <code>if</code> test was false.`,simple:`"...otherwise, wear sunglasses." Exactly one of the two paths happens.`,deep:`<code>else if</code> is just an <code>else</code> whose block is another <code>if</code>, so chains are checked top to bottom and stop at the first true test.`,example:`} else if (pos < 170) {`},{id:`for`,term:`for`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`for`]},short:`Repeats a block a counted number of times, or once for each item in a list.`,simple:`"For each of the 6 bytes, print it." Start at 0, check you're not done, do the job, count up, repeat.`,deep:`<code>for (init; test; step)</code>. The range form <code>for (const auto &amp;card : cards)</code> visits every element of an array or container without an index.`,example:`for (int i = 0; i < 6; i++) Serial.printf("%02X", bytes[i]);`},{id:`while`,term:`while`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`while`]},short:`Repeats a block for as long as its test stays true.`,simple:`"While there are letters in the post box, take one out." When it's empty, stop.`,deep:`The test runs before each round, so the body may run zero times. A <code>while</code> that never ends inside <code>loop()</code> starves the rest of the program; the labs read input with <code>while (Serial.available())</code>, which ends as soon as the buffer is empty.`,example:`while (Serial.available()) {`},{id:`do-while`,term:`do ... while`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`do`]},short:`Like <code>while</code>, but the block always runs at least once before the test.`,simple:`Taste the soup, then decide whether it needs more salt. You always taste first.`,deep:`The test sits after the body and needs a semicolon: <code>do { ... } while (cond);</code>. Handy for "pick a random value until it's a good one".`,example:`do t = 0.1f + (esp_random() % 800) / 1000.0f; while (t < last);`},{id:`switch`,term:`switch`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`switch`]},short:`Picks one of several paths by comparing a value against fixed choices.`,simple:`A lift panel: press 3 and you go to floor 3. Each <code>case</code> is one button.`,deep:`Works on whole numbers and enums, not text. Execution falls through into the next case unless you <code>break</code> or <code>return</code>. With an <code>enum class</code> the compiler warns about cases you forgot.`,example:`switch (esp_reset_reason()) {`},{id:`case`,term:`case`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`case`]},short:`One choice inside a <code>switch</code>: the code to run when the value matches.`,simple:`One button on the lift panel, with its floor written next to it.`,deep:`The label must be a compile-time constant. Several labels can share one body by stacking them; a body without <code>break</code> runs on into the next case.`,example:`case 0: return rgb(v, t, p);`},{id:`default`,term:`default`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`default`]},short:`In a <code>switch</code>, the path taken when no <code>case</code> matched.`,simple:`The "anything else" button.`,deep:`Optional and can sit anywhere in the switch. In a class, <code>= default</code> means something else: ask the compiler to write that constructor or operator for you.`,example:`default: return rgb(v, p, q);`},{id:`break`,term:`break`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`break`]},short:`Leaves the current loop or <code>switch</code> straight away.`,simple:`An emergency exit: stop going round, carry on after the loop.`,deep:`Only leaves the innermost loop or switch. Inside a switch that sits in a loop, <code>break</code> leaves the switch, not the loop.`,example:`case Kind::Fox: drawFox(); break;`},{id:`continue`,term:`continue`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`continue`]},short:`Skips the rest of this round of a loop and starts the next one.`,simple:`"This letter isn't for me: put it aside and open the next."`,deep:`In a <code>for</code> loop the step (<code>i++</code>) still runs before the next test. Often used to skip bad data early instead of nesting <code>if</code>s.`,example:`if (i2s_channel_read(rx, buf, sizeof(buf), &got, 50) != ESP_OK) continue;`},{id:`return`,term:`return`,topic:`flow`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`return`]},short:`Ends the function now, optionally handing a value back to whoever called it.`,simple:`Handing back the finished homework: the job is done and here is the answer.`,deep:`In a <code>void</code> function a bare <code>return;</code> just leaves early, a common way to bail out on bad input (a guard clause). Local variables are destroyed on the way out, which is how <code>std::lock_guard</code> unlocks.`,example:`if (x < 0 || y < 0 || x >= _width || y >= _height) return;`},{id:`setup-loop`,term:`setup() and loop()`,topic:`functions`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`setup`,`loop`]},short:`Every Arduino program has these two: <code>setup()</code> runs once at power-on, <code>loop()</code> then runs forever.`,simple:`Opening a shop: <code>setup()</code> unlocks the door and switches on the lights once; <code>loop()</code> serves customer after customer until closing time (power off).`,deep:`The Arduino core's <code>main</code> starts a FreeRTOS task ("loopTask", core 1, 8 KB stack by default) that calls <code>setup()</code> then <code>loop()</code> over and over. Keep <code>loop()</code> quick and non-blocking: check <code>millis()</code> instead of long <code>delay()</code>s. A lab's <code>lab.loop()</code> is the shell's own per-round work.`,example:`void loop() {`},{id:`lambda`,term:`[ ]( ) { } lambda`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{},short:`A small function written right where it's needed, often passed to another function to call later.`,simple:`A sticky note with instructions you hand to someone: "when a request for / arrives, do this".`,deep:`<code>[capture](params) { body }</code>. The capture list says what it may use from around it: <code>[this]</code> the current object, <code>[&amp;]</code> everything by reference, <code>[]</code> nothing. Web routes, Wi-Fi events and sorting all take lambdas here.`,example:`server_.on("/", HTTP_GET, [](AsyncWebServerRequest *req) { req->send(200); });`},{id:`variadic`,term:`...`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`...`]},short:`Means "any number of extra arguments", like the values a printf pattern needs.`,simple:`"...and so on": the function takes as many extras as you give it.`,deep:`In <code>void warn(const char *fmt, ...)</code> the extras are read with <code>va_list</code> and forwarded to <code>vsnprintf</code>; the <code>[[gnu::format(printf, 2, 3)]]</code> attribute lets the compiler check them against the pattern. In a macro, <code>__VA_ARGS__</code> pastes them in.`,example:`void warn(const char *fmt, ...) {`},{id:`inline`,term:`inline`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`inline`]},short:`Allows a function or variable to be defined in a header that many files include.`,simple:`A recipe printed in every cookbook that needs it: the kitchen agrees they are all the same recipe, not rivals.`,deep:`Its real job is the one-definition rule: an <code>inline</code> function in a <code>.h</code> may appear in many <code>.cpp</code> files without a linker clash. Whether the call is actually inlined is up to the optimiser. <code>constexpr</code> functions and functions defined inside a class are inline automatically.`,example:`inline uint16_t hsv(uint8_t h, uint8_t s, uint8_t v) {`},{id:`operator`,term:`operator`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`operator`]},short:`Teaches a type what a symbol like <code>==</code>, <code>[]</code> or <code>+</code> should do with it.`,simple:`Like teaching a new game piece the existing rules: now <code>a == b</code> works for your own kind of thing too.`,deep:`<code>bool operator==(const Uid &amp;o) const</code> is just a function with a special name; <code>explicit operator bool()</code> lets an object be tested in an <code>if</code>.`,example:`bool operator==(const Uid &o) const;`},{id:`noexcept`,term:`noexcept`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`noexcept`]},short:`A promise that a function will never throw an exception.`,simple:`A sign on the door: "no surprises in here".`,deep:`If it throws anyway, <code>std::terminate</code> ends the program. Standard containers use it to decide whether moving an element is safe. Arduino-ESP32 builds with exceptions available, but the labs never throw.`,example:`uint32_t count() const noexcept { return count_; }`},{id:`override`,term:`override / virtual`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`override`,`virtual`,`final`]},short:`<code>virtual</code> lets a child class replace a function; <code>override</code> marks the replacement.`,simple:`A form with a blank: the library leaves "how to draw one pixel" for you to fill in, and <code>override</code> says "this is my answer to that blank".`,deep:`Calls go through a table of function pointers (the vtable), so <code>Adafruit_GFX</code> code ends up calling our <code>FrameBuffer::drawPixel</code>. <code>override</code> makes the compiler check that a matching virtual exists. NimBLE callbacks such as <code>onConnect</code> work the same way.`,example:`void drawPixel(int16_t x, int16_t y, uint16_t c) override {`},{id:`explicit`,term:`explicit`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`explicit`]},short:`Stops the compiler from silently turning one type into another through a constructor.`,simple:`"Ask first": a number won't become a <code>Button</code> just because a <code>Button</code> can be built from one.`,deep:`Without it, a one-argument constructor is an implicit conversion, so a function taking a <code>BootButton</code> could be called with a plain number. Also used on <code>operator bool</code>.`,example:`explicit BootButton(uint8_t pin) : pin_(pin) {}`},{id:`constructor`,term:`constructor`,topic:`functions`,kind:`language`,match:{},short:`A special function with the class's own name that sets up a new object.`,simple:`The assembly instructions that run once when a new thing comes out of the box.`,deep:`No return type. The list after <code>:</code> (<code>: pin_(pin), name_(name)</code>) fills members before the body runs, in the order they are declared. A <code>~Name()</code> destructor runs when the object goes away, like <code>FrameBuffer</code> freeing its pixels.`,example:`Button(uint8_t pin, const char *name) : pin_(pin), name_(name) {}`},{id:`address-reference`,term:`&`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`&`]},short:`Three jobs: after a type it makes a reference (another name), before a variable it takes its address, between numbers it is bitwise AND.`,simple:`A reference is a nickname: <code>uint8_t &amp;r</code> lets a function write straight into your box. <code>&amp;x</code> is the box's street address. <code>a &amp; b</code> keeps only the bits that are on in both.`,deep:`<code>T &amp;</code> binds to an existing object and can't be null or re-pointed; <code>const T &amp;</code> passes big things without copying. <code>&amp;lock_</code> gives a <code>portMUX_TYPE *</code>. <code>x &amp; 0xFF</code> masks the low byte.`,example:`void wheel(uint8_t pos, uint8_t &r, uint8_t &g, uint8_t &b) {`},{id:`pointer`,term:`*`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{ops:[`*`]},short:`Three jobs: after a type it declares a pointer, before a pointer it follows it, between numbers it multiplies.`,simple:`A pointer is a slip of paper with an address on it. <code>*p</code> means "go to that address and use what's there". Between two numbers, it's just times.`,deep:`Pointers are 4 bytes on the ESP32. <code>uint16_t *buf_</code> holds where the pixels start; <code>buf_[i]</code> is the same as <code>*(buf_ + i)</code>. <code>const char *</code> is a pointer to text you may read but not change.`,example:`uint16_t *pixels() { return buf_; }`},{id:`array`,term:`array [ ]`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{},short:`A fixed row of values of the same type, numbered from 0.`,simple:`An egg box: a fixed number of slots, all the same shape. Slot numbers start at 0, so a 6-slot box has slots 0 to 5.`,deep:`<code>char text[32]</code> reserves 32 bytes right there, with no heap. Nothing checks the index, so writing <code>text[32]</code> silently tramples whatever lives next; <code>sizeof(text)</code> gives the full size in bytes, which is why buffer calls pass it.`,example:`ActEntry ring_[kSize];`},{id:`const`,term:`const`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`const`]},short:`Marks a value as read-only: once set, the code may not change it.`,simple:`A label written in pen, not pencil.`,deep:`Unlike <code>constexpr</code>, the value can come from running code (<code>const uint32_t now = millis();</code>). After a member function's brackets, <code>const</code> promises the function won't change the object. <code>const char *p</code> protects the text; <code>char *const p</code> protects the pointer.`,example:`const char *name() const { return name_; }`},{id:`static`,term:`static`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`static`]},short:`Keeps a variable alive for the whole program, or shares one copy between all objects of a class.`,simple:`Inside a function it's a notebook that stays on the desk between visits, so <code>loop()</code> remembers its last value.`,deep:`Three meanings. A <code>static</code> local is created once and survives between calls. A <code>static</code> member belongs to the class, not each object (the interrupt handler <code>onEdge</code> is static and gets the object through <code>arg</code>). At file level it makes a name private to that file.`,example:`static uint32_t lastHeartbeat = 0;`},{id:`volatile`,term:`volatile`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`volatile`]},short:`Tells the compiler this value can change behind its back, so it must reread it every time.`,simple:`A post box that can fill up while you're not looking: you must walk over and check, not assume it's still empty.`,deep:`Needed for variables written by an interrupt and read by normal code. It is <b>not</b> a lock and not atomic: a 64-bit value can still be torn. For data shared between tasks prefer <code>std::atomic</code>, a mutex or a critical section.`,example:`volatile uint32_t edges_ = 0;  // written by the interrupt`},{id:`sizeof`,term:`sizeof`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`sizeof`]},short:`Tells you how many bytes a type or variable takes up.`,simple:`Like reading the capacity printed on a jar before you pour anything in.`,deep:`Worked out at compile time, it costs nothing at runtime. <code>sizeof(text)</code> of an array is its full byte size, but of a pointer it is just 4. <code>sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0])</code> counts elements.`,example:`strlcpy(c_.why, why, sizeof(c_.why));`},{id:`new-delete`,term:`new / delete`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`new`,`delete`]},short:`<code>new</code> builds an object in free memory (the heap); <code>delete</code> destroys it and gives the memory back.`,simple:`Renting a storage unit: <code>new</code> rents one, <code>delete</code> hands back the key. Forget to hand it back and the space is lost until reboot.`,deep:`Each <code>new</code> needs exactly one <code>delete</code> (<code>new[]</code> needs <code>delete[]</code>). On the ESP32 plain <code>new</code> may land in internal RAM or PSRAM depending on size and build settings; to choose, use <code>heap_caps_malloc</code>. <code>= delete</code> on a function forbids it, such as copying.`,example:`spi_ = new SPIClass(HSPI);  // the S3's SPI3: the screen keeps SPI2 to itself`},{id:`progmem`,term:`PROGMEM`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`PROGMEM`]},short:`Asks for data to stay in flash memory instead of being copied into RAM.`,simple:`Leave the big book on the library shelf and read it there, instead of photocopying it onto your small desk.`,deep:`Essential on AVR Arduinos; on the ESP32 it is defined as nothing, because <code>const</code> data already stays in memory-mapped flash. It is kept for clarity and portability, as with the web page in <code>kPage</code>.`,example:`static const char kPage[] PROGMEM = R"HTML(<!doctype html>...`},{id:`iram-attr`,term:`IRAM_ATTR`,topic:`memory`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`IRAM_ATTR`,`DRAM_ATTR`]},short:`Places a function in the chip's fast internal RAM, so it can run the instant an interrupt fires.`,simple:`Keeping the fire extinguisher by the door instead of in the basement: it must be grabbable immediately.`,deep:`Normal code runs from external flash through a cache, and the cache is switched off while flash is written (NVS, OTA). An interrupt handler living in flash would then crash the chip; <code>IRAM_ATTR</code> keeps it in IRAM. Anything it calls should be in IRAM too, so keep handlers tiny.`,example:`static void IRAM_ATTR onEdge(void *arg) {`},{id:`c-strings`,term:`strlcpy() strcmp() strlen()`,topic:`memory`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`strlcpy`,`strncpy`,`strcpy`,`strcmp`,`strncmp`,`strcasecmp`,`strlen`,`strchr`,`strstr`,`strtol`,`strtoul`,`atoi`]},short:`C's tools for text in <code>char</code> arrays: copy it, compare it, measure it.`,simple:`Working with letter tiles by hand: copy a word onto your row, compare two rows, count the tiles before the stop tile.`,deep:`<code>strlcpy(dst, src, sizeof(dst))</code> copies safely and always ends with <code>'\\0'</code>. <code>strcmp</code> returns 0 when two texts are equal (not true!), so code reads <code>strcmp(a, b) == 0</code>. <code>strlen</code> counts letters, not the final <code>'\\0'</code>.`,example:`if (strcmp(in["type"] | "", "request") != 0) return;`},{id:`memcpy`,term:`memcpy() memset() memcmp()`,topic:`memory`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`memcpy`,`memset`,`memcmp`,`memmove`]},short:`Copy, fill or compare a block of raw bytes.`,simple:`Moving a whole tray of eggs at once instead of one egg at a time.`,deep:`They work on bytes and know nothing about types: the length is always in bytes, so use <code>sizeof</code>. <code>memcpy</code> must not overlap (use <code>memmove</code>); <code>memcmp</code> returns 0 for equal. Card UIDs and BLE payloads are handled this way.`,example:`memcpy(out + 4, ib.uuid, 16);`},{id:`struct`,term:`struct`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`struct`]},short:`Bundles several named values into one new type.`,simple:`A form with labelled fields: one Card has a UID, a name and a colour, and they travel together.`,deep:`Identical to <code>class</code> except members are <code>public</code> by default. The labs use structs for plain data (<code>HttpsResult</code>, <code>Frame</code>) and classes for things with behaviour.`,example:`struct HttpsResult { int status = 0; String body; };`},{id:`class`,term:`class`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`class`]},short:`Defines a new kind of object: the data it keeps and the things it can do.`,simple:`A blueprint for a gadget: what parts it has inside and which buttons are on the outside.`,deep:`Members are <code>private</code> by default. <code>class FrameBuffer : public Adafruit_GFX</code> inherits everything a GFX drawing surface can do. <code>enum class</code> reuses the word for something different: a scoped enum.`,example:`class Button {`},{id:`enum`,term:`enum / enum class`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`enum`]},short:`A list of named choices, like <code>Click</code>, <code>DoubleClick</code>, <code>LongPress</code>.`,simple:`A multiple-choice question: the answer must be one of the options printed on the sheet.`,deep:`<code>enum class Event { ... }</code> is scoped (written <code>Event::Click</code>) and won't turn into an <code>int</code> by accident. <code>: uint8_t</code> picks the storage type. A plain <code>enum</code> leaks its names into the surrounding scope.`,example:`enum class Event { None, Click, DoubleClick, LongPress };`},{id:`namespace`,term:`namespace`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`namespace`]},short:`A named folder for functions and types, so names from different parts never clash.`,simple:`Surnames for code: <code>pal::ink</code> and <code>sv::ink</code> can both exist, like two people called Sam in different families.`,deep:`An unnamed <code>namespace { }</code> makes everything inside private to that <code>.cpp</code> file (internal linkage), as lesson 01 does with its helpers. <code>using namespace x;</code> pulls names out again; avoid it in headers.`,example:`namespace pal { constexpr uint16_t ink = rgb(232, 238, 246); }`},{id:`std`,term:`std`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`std`]},short:`The namespace of the C++ standard library: <code>std::min</code>, <code>std::vector</code>, <code>std::mutex</code>...`,simple:`The toolbox that comes with every C++ compiler. <code>std::</code> says "take this tool from the standard box".`,deep:`Arduino-ESP32 ships a full C++ standard library (GCC's libstdc++), including containers, algorithms, <code>&lt;atomic&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;mutex&gt;</code> on top of FreeRTOS. Watch heap use: containers allocate.`,example:`std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);`},{id:`access`,term:`public / private / protected`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`public`,`private`,`protected`]},short:`Decide who may use a class's members: everyone, only the class itself, or the class and its children.`,simple:`The buttons on the front of a radio are <code>public</code>; the circuit board inside is <code>private</code>.`,deep:`A class keeps its data <code>private</code> (here with a trailing <code>_</code>, as in <code>pin_</code>) and offers <code>public</code> functions, so it can change its insides without breaking callers. In <code>: public Adafruit_GFX</code> the word sets how the parent's members are inherited.`,example:`private:
  uint8_t pin_;`},{id:`this`,term:`this`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`this`]},short:`Inside a member function, a pointer to the object the function is working on.`,simple:`"Me": the object pointing at itself.`,deep:`Usually implicit (<code>pin_</code> means <code>this->pin_</code>). Passed explicitly when a C-style callback needs to find its way back, as in <code>attachInterruptArg(..., onEdge, this, CHANGE)</code> or a task's parameter, and captured by lambdas with <code>[this]</code>.`,example:`attachInterruptArg(digitalPinToInterrupt(pin_), onEdge, this, CHANGE);`},{id:`using`,term:`using`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`using`,`typedef`]},short:`Gives a type a shorter or clearer name.`,simple:`A nickname: say <code>JsonFn</code> instead of the whole long type every time.`,deep:`<code>using JsonFn = std::function&lt;...&gt;;</code> is the modern form of <code>typedef</code> and reads left to right. <code>using namespace std;</code> and <code>using Base::f;</code> are other uses of the same word.`,example:`using JsonFn = std::function<void(AsyncWebServerRequest *, JsonObjectConst)>;`},{id:`union`,term:`union`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`union`]},short:`Several members sharing the same bytes, so only one is meaningful at a time.`,simple:`One parking space with several possible cars: whichever parked last is the one that's there.`,deep:`The union is as big as its largest member. Reading a member other than the one last written is technically undefined in C++ (GCC allows it); <code>memcpy</code> or <code>std::bit_cast</code> are the clean ways to reinterpret bytes.`,example:`union { uint32_t word; uint8_t bytes[4]; } v;`},{id:`extern`,term:`extern`,topic:`classes`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`extern`]},short:`Says a variable exists, but is defined somewhere else in the program.`,simple:`"The key is in the other room": you can talk about it here, and the linker fetches it later.`,deep:`A declaration without a definition. The HTTPS client uses it to reach the Mozilla certificate bundle that the build links into flash, by its linker symbol names.`,example:`extern const uint8_t kHttpsCaBundleStart[] asm("_binary_x509_crt_bundle_start");`},{id:`constexpr`,term:`constexpr`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`constexpr`,`consteval`,`constinit`]},short:`A value fixed when the program is compiled, so it can never change while the board runs.`,simple:`Like a number printed on the box instead of written on a sticky note: settled before the board ever powers up.`,deep:`<code>constexpr</code> variables are computed by the compiler and cost no RAM at runtime; a <code>constexpr</code> function such as <code>rgb()</code> can run at compile time when its inputs are constants. The labs name these <code>kSomething</code>.`,example:`constexpr uint32_t kHeartbeatMs = 5000;`},{id:`static-cast`,term:`static_cast`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`static_cast`]},short:`Converts a value to another type on purpose, where you can see it.`,simple:`Pouring a drink into a different glass, deliberately and in plain sight.`,deep:`Checked by the compiler and free at runtime for number types. Common here: <code>static_cast&lt;unsigned long&gt;</code> to match <code>%lu</code>, and turning a <code>void *</code> back into the object it was. The short form <code>uint8_t(x)</code> does the same for simple types.`,example:`static_cast<unsigned long>(millis() / 1000)`},{id:`reinterpret-cast`,term:`reinterpret_cast`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`reinterpret_cast`]},short:`Treats the same bytes as a completely different type, without changing them.`,simple:`Reading a page of numbers as if it were letters: the ink is the same, only how you read it changes.`,deep:`No conversion happens; it just relabels a pointer (for example <code>uint8_t *</code> to <code>const char *</code> for a byte buffer of text). Easy to misuse: alignment and strict aliasing rules still apply.`,example:`reinterpret_cast<const char *>(p + i + 8)`},{id:`const-cast`,term:`const_cast`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`const_cast`,`dynamic_cast`]},short:`Removes <code>const</code> from a pointer so it can be passed to an old-style function.`,simple:`Borrowing a pen-written label for a function that only accepts pencil ones: you promise it won't actually write.`,deep:`Only safe when the object was not really const to begin with, or when the callee does not modify it (many C APIs just forgot <code>const</code>). Writing through it to truly const data is undefined.`,example:`hid_->setReportMap(const_cast<uint8_t *>(kReportMap), sizeof(kReportMap));`},{id:`template`,term:`template / typename`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`template`,`typename`]},short:`Writes a function or class once for any type; the compiler makes a copy for each type used.`,simple:`A cookie cutter: one shape, any dough. <code>T</code> stands in for the dough until you choose it.`,deep:`<code>template &lt;typename F&gt; void withChip(F &amp;&amp;fn)</code> accepts any callable, including lambdas, with no runtime cost. Every new type means a new compiled copy, so heavy use grows the firmware. Angle brackets supply the type: <code>std::vector&lt;Card&gt;</code>.`,example:`template <typename F>`},{id:`std-min-max`,term:`std::min() std::max()`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`std::min`,`std::max`,`std::minmax`]},short:`Give back the smaller or the larger of two values.`,simple:`"Whichever is lower" and "whichever is higher": handy for keeping numbers in range.`,deep:`Both arguments must be the same type, or you name it: <code>std::min&lt;uint32_t&gt;(n, 320)</code>. <code>std::max(0, n - 9)</code> stops a value going below zero.`,example:`const int from = std::max(0, n - 9);`},{id:`std-clamp`,term:`std::clamp()`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`std::clamp`]},short:`Squeezes a value into a range: below the low end gives the low end, above the high end gives the high end.`,simple:`A child gate on the stairs: you can move around, but not past the gates at either end.`,deep:`C++17, from <code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code>. All three arguments must share one type, hence the <code>0.0f</code> and <code>1.0f</code>. Arduino's <code>constrain()</code> does the same job.`,example:`const float level = std::clamp((temp - 20) / 60, 0.0f, 1.0f);`},{id:`std-algorithms`,term:`std::fill_n() std::copy() std::sort()`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`std::fill_n`,`std::fill`,`std::copy`,`std::copy_n`,`std::sort`,`std::reverse`,`std::max_element`,`std::min_element`,`std::nth_element`,`std::find`,`std::find_if`,`std::count_if`,`std::remove_if`]},short:`Ready-made loops from the standard library: fill, copy, sort, find the biggest...`,simple:`Kitchen gadgets: you could chop by hand, but the gadget is quicker and doesn't slip.`,deep:`From <code>&lt;algorithm&gt;</code>, they work on any range given as a start and an end pointer (or iterator), with the end one past the last item. <code>std::fill_n(buf_, n, c)</code> paints a whole screen; <code>std::sort</code> takes a lambda saying what "comes first" means.`,example:`std::fill_n(buf_, size_t(_width) * _height, c);`},{id:`std-move`,term:`std::move()`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`std::move`,`std::forward`,`std::swap`]},short:`Hands an object's contents over to another instead of copying them.`,simple:`Moving house with the boxes as they are, instead of buying a copy of everything for the new place.`,deep:`It is only a cast to an rvalue reference (<code>T &amp;&amp;</code>); the actual move is done by the receiving type's move constructor. Afterwards the source is valid but empty-ish, so don't rely on its value. Cheap for <code>String</code>, <code>std::vector</code> and <code>std::function</code>.`,example:`outbox_.push_back(std::move(text));`},{id:`std-vector`,term:`std::vector`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`vector`,`deque`,`initializer_list`]},short:`A list that can grow and shrink, holding items of one type.`,simple:`An expanding file folder: add sheets and it makes room, and you can still say "give me sheet 3".`,deep:`Stores items in one contiguous heap block and doubles it when full, which moves (and may fragment) memory. The labs fill vectors outside time-critical paths, for example copying the card list for the screen. <code>v.size()</code>, <code>v.push_back(x)</code>, <code>v[i]</code>.`,example:`std::vector<door::Card> list() {`},{id:`std-function`,term:`std::function`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`function`]},short:`A box that can hold any function or lambda with a given shape, to call later.`,simple:`A slot for a sticky note of instructions: any note fits, as long as it takes the same inputs.`,deep:`<code>std::function&lt;void(Card &amp;)&gt;</code> takes a <code>Card &amp;</code> and returns nothing. Flexible but heavier than a template: calling it is indirect and large captures go on the heap. Good for callbacks stored in a class.`,example:`const std::function<void(Card &)> &change`},{id:`std-atomic`,term:`std::atomic`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`atomic`]},short:`A variable that two tasks can read and write at the same time without ever seeing half a change.`,simple:`A single light switch everybody can flip: no matter who touches it, it's always clearly on or off.`,deep:`<code>std::atomic&lt;bool&gt;</code> and <code>std::atomic&lt;uint32_t&gt;</code> are lock-free on the ESP32. Use them for one small value such as a flag or counter; for several values that must change together, use a mutex.`,example:`std::atomic<bool> resetWanted_{false};`},{id:`memory-order`,term:`std::memory_order`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`memory_order_acquire`,`memory_order_release`,`memory_order_relaxed`,`memory_order_seq_cst`]},short:`Says how strictly an atomic read or write must line up with the memory around it.`,simple:`"Release" is like posting a letter only after it's sealed; "acquire" is opening it before reading. Together they guarantee the reader sees everything the writer prepared.`,deep:`<code>relaxed</code> only makes the one value atomic. A <code>release</code> store paired with an <code>acquire</code> load makes everything written before the store visible after the load, across the ESP32's two cores. The default (<code>seq_cst</code>) is the strictest.`,example:`written_.store(w, std::memory_order_release);`},{id:`std-mutex`,term:`std::mutex`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`mutex`]},short:`A lock that lets only one task at a time into a piece of code or data.`,simple:`The single key to a shared bathroom: whoever holds it goes in, everyone else waits by the door.`,deep:`On Arduino-ESP32 it is built on a FreeRTOS mutex, so a waiting task sleeps instead of spinning. Never lock it in an interrupt. Lock it through <code>std::lock_guard</code> so it is always unlocked, even on an early <code>return</code>.`,example:`std::mutex mutex_;`},{id:`lock-guard`,term:`std::lock_guard`,topic:`modern`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`lock_guard`,`scoped_lock`,`unique_lock`]},short:`Locks a mutex when created and unlocks it automatically at the end of the block.`,simple:`A self-returning key: it takes the bathroom key as you go in and puts it back by itself when you leave, however you leave.`,deep:`RAII: the constructor calls <code>lock()</code>, the destructor calls <code>unlock()</code> when the variable goes out of scope. That makes it impossible to forget the unlock on an early <code>return</code>. Keep the block short.`,example:`std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);`},{id:`decltype`,term:`decltype`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`decltype`]},short:`Asks the compiler "what type is this expression?" and uses the answer as a type.`,simple:`"Give me a box exactly like that one."`,deep:`Evaluated at compile time; the expression is never run. Useful in templates and for naming a lambda's type.`,example:`decltype(cards_[0]) first = cards_[0];`},{id:`static-assert`,term:`static_assert`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`static_assert`]},short:`A check that runs while compiling; if it fails, the build stops with your message.`,simple:`A gatekeeper at the factory: a part that doesn't fit never leaves the building.`,deep:`Costs nothing at runtime. Typical use: check that a packed struct really has the byte size a protocol expects, or that a table has one entry per enum value.`,example:`static_assert(sizeof(Report) == 8, "HID report must be 8 bytes");`},{id:`mutable`,term:`mutable`,topic:`modern`,kind:`language`,match:{words:[`mutable`]},short:`Lets a member change even inside a <code>const</code> function, for bookkeeping that isn't part of the object's value.`,simple:`A scratch pad clipped to a sealed document: the document stays the same, the notes can change.`,deep:`Typical for a mutex or a cache inside an object whose read functions are <code>const</code>: locking changes the mutex, but not what the object means. On a lambda, <code>mutable</code> lets it change its own captured copies.`,example:`mutable std::mutex mutex_;`},{id:`include`,term:`#include`,topic:`preprocessor`,kind:`language`,match:{directives:[`#include`]},short:`Pastes another file in here, so this file can use what it declares.`,simple:`Like saying "see the attached recipe card" at the top of a recipe: the ingredients it lists are now available.`,deep:`<code>&lt;Arduino.h&gt;</code> in angle brackets searches the library and system paths; <code>"pins.h"</code> in quotes looks next to this file (and the project's <code>include/</code>) first. It is plain text pasting, done before compiling.`,example:`#include <Arduino.h>`},{id:`pragma-once`,term:`#pragma once`,topic:`preprocessor`,kind:`language`,match:{directives:[`#pragma`]},short:`Makes sure a header file is only pasted in once, however many files include it.`,simple:`A "you've already read this" bookmark: the second time, the page is skipped.`,deep:`Supported by GCC and every major compiler; it replaces the older <code>#ifndef X / #define X / #endif</code> include guard. Without it, a struct defined twice would be a compile error.`,example:`#pragma once`},{id:`define`,term:`#define`,topic:`preprocessor`,kind:`language`,match:{directives:[`#define`,`#undef`]},short:`Creates a name that gets replaced by some text before the code is compiled.`,simple:`Find-and-replace done by the computer just before reading your code: every <code>PIN_RGB</code> becomes <code>48</code>.`,deep:`Macros know nothing about types or scope. <code>pins.h</code> uses them so a build flag like <code>-DPIN_RGB=38</code> can override a pin; for ordinary constants prefer <code>constexpr</code>. A macro with brackets, like <code>BLE_NOTE(kind, ...)</code>, pastes its arguments in.`,example:`#define PIN_RGB 48`},{id:`conditional-compile`,term:`#ifndef / #if / #endif`,topic:`preprocessor`,kind:`language`,match:{directives:[`#ifndef`,`#ifdef`,`#if`,`#elif`,`#else`,`#endif`]},short:`Keeps or drops lines of code before compiling, depending on whether a name is defined.`,simple:`"If nobody chose a pin yet, use 48." The lines that don't apply are cut out before the compiler ever sees them.`,deep:`<code>#ifndef PIN_RGB</code> ... <code>#endif</code> lets <code>platformio.ini</code> build flags win over the defaults in <code>pins.h</code>. <code>#if</code> tests a constant expression; every <code>#if</code>/<code>#ifdef</code>/<code>#ifndef</code> needs a matching <code>#endif</code>.`,example:`#ifndef PIN_RGB
#define PIN_RGB 48
#endif`},{id:`serial-begin`,term:`Serial.begin()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`Serial.begin`]},short:`Opens the serial line to your computer at a chosen speed, so the board can print messages.`,simple:`Picking up the phone and agreeing how fast you'll both talk. 115200 is the speed the serial monitor listens at.`,deep:`Speed in baud (bits per second); it must match <code>monitor_speed</code> in <code>platformio.ini</code>. In these labs <code>Serial</code> is UART0, reached through the USB-to-serial chip on the <code>COM</code> port, so nothing needs to wait for USB to enumerate.`,example:`Serial.begin(115200);`},{id:`serial-printf`,term:`Serial.printf()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`Serial.printf`]},short:`Prints a line to the serial monitor, filling in values with <code>%</code> codes.`,simple:`A form letter: <code>"%d cores"</code> has a blank, and the number you pass fills it.`,deep:`Formats with <code>vsnprintf</code> then writes to UART0 at 115200 baud (about 11.5 KB/s, so long prints slow a loop down). Codes: <code>%d</code> int, <code>%u</code> unsigned, <code>%lu</code> unsigned long, <code>%.1f</code> float, <code>%s</code> C string (use <code>.c_str()</code> for a <code>String</code>), <code>%02X</code> two-digit hex. Add <code>\\n</code> yourself.`,example:`Serial.printf("| chip %s rev %d\\n", ESP.getChipModel(), ESP.getChipRevision());`},{id:`serial-print`,term:`Serial.print() / println()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`Serial.println`,`Serial.print`,`Serial.write`]},short:`Prints text or a number to the serial monitor; <code>println</code> also starts a new line.`,simple:`Saying something out loud to your computer. <code>println</code> is like pressing Enter afterwards.`,deep:`Accepts text, numbers and <code>String</code>; <code>Serial.print(x, HEX)</code> prints in hex. It writes into a transmit buffer and only waits when that buffer is full.`,example:`Serial.println("+---------------- ESP32 identity card ----------------+");`},{id:`serial-read`,term:`Serial.available() / read()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`Serial.available`,`Serial.read`,`Serial.readStringUntil`,`Serial.peek`,`Serial.flush`]},short:`<code>available()</code> says how many typed letters are waiting; <code>read()</code> takes the next one.`,simple:`Checking the letterbox: <code>available()</code> tells you how many letters are in it, <code>read()</code> takes one out.`,deep:`Both return immediately; <code>read()</code> gives -1 when nothing is waiting. The labs collect letters into a buffer until a newline, then run the command, so typing never blocks the screen. <code>flush()</code> waits until everything printed has been sent.`,example:`while (Serial.available()) { const char c = Serial.read(); }`},{id:`pin-mode`,term:`pinMode()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`pinMode`]},short:`Sets a GPIO pin up as an input or an output before you use it.`,simple:`Deciding whether a door is for coming in (reading a button) or going out (lighting an LED).`,deep:`Configures the GPIO matrix for that pin. <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> also turns on a weak internal resistor to 3.3 V, so a button to GND needs no extra parts. Avoid the strapping, flash and PSRAM pins listed at the top of <code>pins.h</code>.`,example:`pinMode(pin_, INPUT_PULLUP);`},{id:`pin-modes`,term:`INPUT / OUTPUT / INPUT_PULLUP`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`INPUT`,`OUTPUT`,`INPUT_PULLUP`,`INPUT_PULLDOWN`,`OUTPUT_OPEN_DRAIN`]},short:`The ways a pin can be set up: listen, drive, or listen with a built-in resistor.`,simple:`<code>OUTPUT</code> is a tap you open and close. <code>INPUT</code> is a gauge you read. <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> is a gauge that rests at "high" until something pulls it down.`,deep:`A floating <code>INPUT</code> picks up noise; <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code>/<code>INPUT_PULLDOWN</code> enable the ESP32's weak internal resistors (tens of kilo-ohms). That's why pressed buttons read <code>LOW</code> in these labs.`,example:`pinMode(PIN_LED, OUTPUT);`},{id:`digital-write`,term:`digitalWrite()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`digitalWrite`]},short:`Switches an output pin on (3.3 V) or off (0 V).`,simple:`Flicking a light switch: <code>HIGH</code> is on, <code>LOW</code> is off.`,deep:`The ESP32-S3 pins are 3.3 V, never 5 V. Each pin can source or sink around 20 mA by default (adjustable with <code>gpio_set_drive_capability</code>), so an LED always needs a series resistor.`,example:`digitalWrite(PIN_LED, HIGH);`},{id:`digital-read`,term:`digitalRead()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`digitalRead`]},short:`Reads whether an input pin is high (near 3.3 V) or low (near 0 V).`,simple:`Asking a doorbell button: are you pressed right now?`,deep:`Returns <code>HIGH</code> (1) or <code>LOW</code> (0). With <code>INPUT_PULLUP</code> a pressed button reads <code>LOW</code>, hence <code>== LOW</code> meaning "pressed". Mechanical contacts bounce for a few ms, which <code>Button</code> filters out.`,example:`const bool level = digitalRead(pin_) == LOW;`},{id:`high-low`,term:`HIGH / LOW`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`HIGH`,`LOW`]},short:`The two levels of a digital pin: <code>HIGH</code> is 3.3 V, <code>LOW</code> is 0 V.`,simple:`On and off, voltage-style.`,deep:`They are simply 1 and 0. On the ESP32 an input reads <code>HIGH</code> above roughly three quarters of 3.3 V and <code>LOW</code> below a quarter; in between is not guaranteed.`,example:`digitalWrite(PIN_LED, LOW);`},{id:`analog-read`,term:`analogRead()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`analogRead`,`analogReadMilliVolts`]},short:`Measures the voltage on a pin as a number from 0 to 4095.`,simple:`A dimmer reading instead of an on/off switch: how far up is the voltage, on a scale of 0 to 4095?`,deep:`The ESP32-S3 ADC is 12-bit. Use ADC1 pins (GPIO 1-10): ADC2 is shared with Wi-Fi. One call is one slow sample, which is why the sound labs use the ADC's continuous mode with DMA instead.`,example:`const int raw = analogRead(PIN_MIC_AO);  // GPIO 6, ADC1`},{id:`millis`,term:`millis()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`millis`]},short:`How many milliseconds have passed since the board started.`,simple:`A stopwatch that starts at power-on and never stops. Compare it with an earlier reading to know how long it's been.`,deep:`Returns a 32-bit <code>unsigned long</code> derived from the 64-bit <code>esp_timer</code>. It wraps to 0 after about 49.7 days, and <code>millis() - last >= interval</code> stays correct across the wrap thanks to unsigned maths. This is how loops do things "every N ms" without <code>delay()</code>.`,example:`if (millis() - lastHeartbeat >= kHeartbeatMs) {`},{id:`micros`,term:`micros()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`micros`]},short:`How many microseconds (millionths of a second) have passed since the board started.`,simple:`The same stopwatch as <code>millis()</code>, read a thousand times more finely.`,deep:`32-bit, so it wraps after about 71.6 minutes; fine for timing short things, use <code>esp_timer_get_time()</code> for a 64-bit clock that never wraps in practice.`,example:`const uint32_t t0 = micros();`},{id:`delay`,term:`delay()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`delay`]},short:`Pauses the current code for a number of milliseconds.`,simple:`Taking a nap: nothing else in <code>loop()</code> happens until you wake up.`,deep:`On the ESP32 it calls <code>vTaskDelay</code>, so the CPU is free for other tasks (Wi-Fi, BLE, the sampler) while this one sleeps. The FreeRTOS tick is 1 ms here. Long delays still freeze buttons and the screen, so the labs mostly use <code>millis()</code> checks.`,example:`delay(1500);  // give the serial monitor a moment to connect`},{id:`delay-microseconds`,term:`delayMicroseconds()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`delayMicroseconds`,`ets_delay_us`]},short:`Waits a tiny number of microseconds, for hardware that needs very short pauses.`,simple:`Holding your breath for a blink: too short to nap, so you just wait in place.`,deep:`A busy-wait: the CPU spins and other tasks on this core don't run. Fine for a few hundred microseconds (letting a pull-up settle), never for long waits.`,example:`delayMicroseconds(200);  // let the weak internal pull settle`},{id:`ledc-attach`,term:`ledcAttach()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`ledcAttach`,`ledcAttachChannel`,`ledcDetach`]},short:`Turns a pin into a PWM output with a chosen frequency and resolution, for dimming LEDs or backlights.`,simple:`Setting up a very fast light switch that flicks on and off thousands of times a second, so the eye sees "half bright".`,deep:`Arduino-ESP32 3.x API on the LEDC peripheral: <code>ledcAttach(pin, freq, bits)</code> returns <code>false</code> if no channel or frequency fits. 5 kHz at 8 bits gives duty values 0-255 with no visible flicker.`,example:`ledcAttach(PIN_TFT_BL, 5000, 8);`},{id:`ledc-write`,term:`ledcWrite()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`ledcWrite`,`ledcWriteTone`,`ledcFade`]},short:`Sets how much of each PWM cycle the pin stays on: 0 is off, the top value is fully on.`,simple:`Turning the dimmer knob on that fast switch.`,deep:`In Arduino-ESP32 3.x it takes the pin (2.x took a channel). The duty range is 0 to 2^bits - 1 (0-255 for 8 bits) from <code>ledcAttach</code>; the LEDC hardware keeps the waveform running with no CPU work.`,example:`ledcWrite(PIN_TFT_BL, 255);`},{id:`rgb-led-write`,term:`rgbLedWrite()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`rgbLedWrite`,`neopixelWrite`]},short:`Sets the colour of the board's built-in RGB LED from red, green and blue values (0-255 each).`,simple:`Mixing paint: a bit of red, lots of green, no blue. The LED shows the mix.`,deep:`The on-board LED is a WS2812 that takes colour as a precisely timed pulse train; Arduino-ESP32 generates it with the RMT peripheral. It is very bright, so the labs scale values down. Was called <code>neopixelWrite</code> in older cores.`,example:`rgbLedWrite(PIN_RGB, r, g, b);`},{id:`attach-interrupt`,term:`attachInterrupt()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`attachInterruptArg`,`attachInterrupt`,`detachInterrupt`],words:[`CHANGE`,`RISING`,`FALLING`,`ONLOW`,`ONHIGH`]},short:`Runs a function the instant a pin changes, even while the rest of the code is busy.`,simple:`A doorbell instead of peeking out the window: the board gets interrupted the moment someone presses.`,deep:`<code>CHANGE</code>, <code>RISING</code> or <code>FALLING</code> chooses which edges fire. <code>attachInterruptArg</code> also passes a <code>void *</code> (here <code>this</code>) so a static handler can find its object. Handlers must be short, marked <code>IRAM_ATTR</code>, and only touch <code>volatile</code> or atomic data.`,example:`attachInterruptArg(digitalPinToInterrupt(pin_), onEdge, this, CHANGE);`},{id:`digital-pin-to-interrupt`,term:`digitalPinToInterrupt()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`digitalPinToInterrupt`]},short:`Turns a pin number into the interrupt number <code>attachInterrupt</code> expects.`,simple:`Looking up which doorbell wire belongs to which door.`,deep:`On the ESP32 every GPIO can interrupt and the number is the pin itself, but writing it keeps code portable to boards where they differ.`,example:`digitalPinToInterrupt(PIN_BUTTON)`},{id:`random`,term:`random()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`random`,`randomSeed`]},short:`Picks a whole number at random: <code>random(40, 200)</code> gives 40 up to 199.`,simple:`Rolling dice whose sides you choose. The top number is never rolled.`,deep:`<code>random(max)</code> gives 0 to max-1, <code>random(min, max)</code> min to max-1. <code>randomSeed(esp_random())</code> starts the sequence from a hardware random number, so every boot looks different.`,example:`spawn(random(40, w - 40), h, random(-25, 25), -random(150, 205), 2.0f, uint8_t(random(256)), true);`},{id:`constrain`,term:`constrain()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`constrain`]},short:`Keeps a value between a lowest and a highest allowed value.`,simple:`Bumpers on a bowling lane: the ball can roll anywhere between them, never past.`,deep:`Arduino's version of <code>std::clamp</code>: <code>constrain(x, lo, hi)</code>. MIDI notes and velocities must stay 0-127, so the BLE MIDI lab clamps them.`,example:`return uint8_t(constrain(note, 0, 127));`},{id:`min-max`,term:`min() / max()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`min`,`max`]},short:`Arduino's shorthand for the smaller or the larger of two values.`,simple:`"Whichever is smaller" or "whichever is bigger". <code>max(2, s / 6)</code> means "at least 2".`,deep:`Give both arguments the same type to avoid surprises. Elsewhere the labs write <code>std::min</code>/<code>std::max</code>, which also accept an explicit type: <code>std::min&lt;uint32_t&gt;(n, 320)</code>.`,example:`fb.fillEllipse(cx, ground + 2, rx, max(2, s / 6), pal::dim);`},{id:`string-c-str`,term:`.c_str()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`c_str`]},short:`Gives a <code>String</code>'s text as a plain C string, for functions that want <code>const char *</code>.`,simple:`Taking the letters out of their stretchy notebook and laying them on the table, for someone who only reads tiles.`,deep:`The pointer is only valid while the <code>String</code> lives and isn't changed; never keep it from a temporary such as <code>a.toString().c_str()</code> beyond that line. Needed for <code>%s</code> in printf.`,example:`WiFi.begin(ssid_.c_str(), pass_.isEmpty() ? nullptr : pass_.c_str());`},{id:`string-is-empty`,term:`.isEmpty() / .length()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`isEmpty`,`length`]},short:`Ask a <code>String</code> whether it has no letters, or how many letters it has.`,simple:`Is the notebook page blank? How many letters are on it?`,deep:`<code>length()</code> counts characters (bytes, so an emoji counts as several) and doesn't include the hidden <code>'\\0'</code>. <code>isEmpty()</code> is the same as <code>length() == 0</code> and reads better.`,example:`if (line.isEmpty()) return;`},{id:`string-substring`,term:`.substring() / .indexOf()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`substring`,`indexOf`,`lastIndexOf`,`charAt`]},short:`<code>indexOf</code> finds where some text appears; <code>substring</code> cuts out a piece.`,simple:`Finding a word in a sentence with your finger, then snipping out the part you need with scissors.`,deep:`Positions count from 0. <code>substring(from)</code> goes to the end, <code>substring(from, to)</code> stops just before <code>to</code>. <code>indexOf</code> returns -1 when nothing is found. Each <code>substring</code> makes a new <code>String</code> on the heap.`,example:`const long n = line.substring(6).toInt();`},{id:`string-starts-with`,term:`.startsWith() / .endsWith()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`startsWith`,`endsWith`,`equalsIgnoreCase`]},short:`Checks whether a <code>String</code> begins (or ends) with some text.`,simple:`"Does this command start with 'vol '?" A quick yes or no.`,deep:`Case-sensitive, which is why commands are lower-cased first (<code>lower.startsWith(...)</code>). It's how every lab's serial command parser recognises its words.`,example:`if (lower.startsWith("scene ")) {`},{id:`string-trim`,term:`.trim() / .toLowerCase()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`trim`,`toLowerCase`,`toUpperCase`,`replace`]},short:`Tidy text in place: remove spaces at the ends, change letter case, or swap one piece of text for another.`,simple:`Straightening a note before reading it: no stray spaces, all in small letters, so "Help " and "help" mean the same.`,deep:`These change the <code>String</code> itself and return nothing, so call them on a copy you own: <code>String lower = line; lower.toLowerCase();</code>. <code>trim</code> also removes the <code>\\r</code> some terminals send.`,example:`line.trim();`},{id:`string-to-int`,term:`.toInt() / .toFloat()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`toInt`,`toFloat`,`toDouble`]},short:`Turns text like <code>"42"</code> into the number 42.`,simple:`Reading the digits on a note and typing them into a calculator.`,deep:`Returns a <code>long</code> (or <code>float</code>). Text that doesn't start with a number gives 0, so a typed <code>0</code> and nonsense look the same; check the text when that matters.`,example:`const long n = arg.toInt();`},{id:`spi-begin`,term:`SPI.begin()`,topic:`arduino`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`SPI.begin`,`SPI.beginTransaction`,`SPI.endTransaction`,`SPI.transfer`],words:[`SPIClass`,`SPISettings`]},short:`Starts the SPI bus on the pins you choose, so the board can talk fast to the screen or the RFID reader.`,simple:`Laying a private phone line with separate wires for talking, listening and the beat both sides keep time to.`,deep:`<code>SPI.begin(sck, miso, mosi, ss)</code>; -1 skips a pin (the display has no MISO). <code>SPI</code> is the S3's SPI2 (FSPI); the RFID track builds a second <code>SPIClass</code> on SPI3 so the two never share wires or timing.`,example:`SPI.begin(PIN_TFT_SCLK, -1, PIN_TFT_MOSI, PIN_TFT_CS);`},{id:`log-macros`,term:`log_i() log_w() log_e()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`log_i`,`log_w`,`log_e`,`log_d`,`log_v`,`log_n`]},short:`Print a tagged log line: <code>i</code> info, <code>w</code> warning, <code>e</code> error, <code>d</code> debug.`,simple:`Notes in a ship's logbook, each with a stamp saying how serious it is.`,deep:`Arduino-ESP32 macros with printf-style patterns. Each line is prefixed with level, file, line and function, and lines above <code>CORE_DEBUG_LEVEL</code> (3 = info in <code>platformio.ini</code>) are removed at compile time, so <code>log_d</code> costs nothing here. Output goes to the same serial port as <code>Serial</code>.`,example:`log_i("alive for %lus, hue=%u", static_cast<unsigned long>(millis() / 1000), hue);`},{id:`esp-info`,term:`ESP.getChipModel() and friends`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`ESP.getChipModel`,`ESP.getChipRevision`,`ESP.getChipCores`,`ESP.getCpuFreqMHz`,`ESP.getFlashChipSize`,`ESP.getPsramSize`,`ESP.getSdkVersion`,`ESP.getEfuseMac`]},short:`Ask the chip about itself: model, cores, speed, flash and PSRAM size, MAC address.`,simple:`The board reading its own ID card out loud.`,deep:`Lesson 01 expects <i>ESP32-S3</i>, 2 cores at 240 MHz, 16 MB flash (the N16) and about 8 MB PSRAM (the R8). <code>getEfuseMac()</code> returns the factory MAC from eFuse as a <code>uint64_t</code>, lowest byte first.`,example:`ESP.getChipModel(), ESP.getChipCores(), ESP.getCpuFreqMHz()`},{id:`free-heap`,term:`ESP.getFreeHeap()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`ESP.getFreeHeap`,`ESP.getFreePsram`,`ESP.getMinFreeHeap`,`ESP.getMaxAllocHeap`,`heap_caps_get_free_size`,`heap_caps_get_total_size`,`heap_caps_get_largest_free_block`]},short:`How much memory is still free for new allocations.`,simple:`Checking how much room is left in the cupboard before buying more.`,deep:`<code>ESP.getFreeHeap()</code> counts internal RAM; <code>heap_caps_get_free_size(MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM)</code> counts PSRAM. Free is not the same as usable: <code>heap_caps_get_largest_free_block</code> tells you the biggest single piece, which is what a frame buffer needs.`,example:`const size_t psFree = heap_caps_get_free_size(MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM);`},{id:`esp-restart`,term:`ESP.restart()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`ESP.restart`,`esp_restart`]},short:`Restarts the chip from scratch, as if you had pressed the reset button.`,simple:`Switching it off and on again, from inside the program.`,deep:`A software reset: RAM is cleared, NVS (Preferences) survives, and <code>esp_reset_reason()</code> reports <code>ESP_RST_SW</code> next time. Used after changing a saved setting that only applies at start-up.`,example:`if (state.rebootAt && now >= state.rebootAt) ESP.restart();`},{id:`esp-reset-reason`,term:`esp_reset_reason()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`esp_reset_reason`]},short:`Tells you why the board last started: power-on, reset button, crash, brown-out...`,simple:`Waking up and asking "did I just sleep, or did I fall over?"`,deep:`Returns an <code>esp_reset_reason_t</code> such as <code>ESP_RST_POWERON</code>, <code>ESP_RST_SW</code>, <code>ESP_RST_PANIC</code>, <code>ESP_RST_TASK_WDT</code> or <code>ESP_RST_BROWNOUT</code> (supply voltage dipped). Printing it at boot makes crashes visible.`,example:`switch (esp_reset_reason()) {`},{id:`esp-timer-get-time`,term:`esp_timer_get_time()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`esp_timer_get_time`]},short:`Microseconds since boot, from a 64-bit clock that never runs out.`,simple:`A very precise stopwatch: good for measuring how long one screen frame took to draw.`,deep:`Returns <code>int64_t</code> from the ESP-IDF high-resolution timer; safe to call from an interrupt. <code>millis()</code> and <code>micros()</code> are built on it. The button handler keeps only the low 32 bits so one read is a single step.`,example:`const int64_t t0 = esp_timer_get_time();`},{id:`esp-timer`,term:`esp_timer_create()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`esp_timer_create`,`esp_timer_start_periodic`,`esp_timer_start_once`,`esp_timer_stop`,`esp_timer_delete`],words:[`esp_timer_handle_t`,`esp_timer_create_args_t`]},short:`Sets up a callback that ESP-IDF runs after a delay or on a steady beat, with microsecond timing.`,simple:`A kitchen timer that rings every 125 ms, whatever the rest of the code is doing.`,deep:`Callbacks run in the high-priority <code>esp_timer</code> task, not in <code>loop()</code>, so keep them short and share data safely. Periods are in microseconds.`,example:`if (esp_timer_create(&args, &replay.timer) != ESP_OK) return;`},{id:`esp-random`,term:`esp_random()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`esp_random`,`esp_fill_random`]},short:`A random 32-bit number from the chip's hardware random number generator.`,simple:`Dice rolled by physics inside the chip rather than by a formula.`,deep:`Truly random while Wi-Fi or Bluetooth is running (radio noise feeds it); before that, treat it as only pseudo-random. <code>esp_fill_random(buf, n)</code> fills a buffer, as the vault lab does for its secret key.`,example:`card.nonce = esp_random();`},{id:`heap-caps-malloc`,term:`heap_caps_malloc()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`heap_caps_malloc`,`heap_caps_calloc`,`heap_caps_realloc`,`heap_caps_free`,`ps_malloc`],words:[`MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM`,`MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL`,`MALLOC_CAP_8BIT`,`MALLOC_CAP_DMA`,`MALLOC_CAP_DEFAULT`]},short:`Asks for a block of memory from a chosen kind of RAM: fast internal or big external PSRAM.`,simple:`Choosing between the small drawer at your desk (quick to reach) and the big storeroom down the hall (roomy but slower).`,deep:`The S3 has about 512 KB of internal SRAM and here 8 MB of octal PSRAM. <code>MALLOC_CAP_INTERNAL</code> or <code>MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM</code> picks the pool; the result is <code>nullptr</code> if no block is big enough. Free it with <code>heap_caps_free</code>.`,example:`buf_ = static_cast<uint16_t *>(heap_caps_malloc(bytes, MALLOC_CAP_SPIRAM | MALLOC_CAP_8BIT));`},{id:`gpio-drive`,term:`gpio_set_drive_capability()`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`gpio_set_drive_capability`],words:[`gpio_num_t`,`GPIO_DRIVE_CAP_0`,`GPIO_DRIVE_CAP_1`,`GPIO_DRIVE_CAP_2`,`GPIO_DRIVE_CAP_3`]},short:`Chooses how strongly an output pin pushes current, from weak to strong.`,simple:`Setting how hard a tap can push water: a gentle trickle or full pressure.`,deep:`An ESP-IDF call (it needs a <code>gpio_num_t</code>, hence the cast). Levels 0-3 are roughly 5, 10, 20 (the default) and 40 mA. A weaker drive makes edges gentler and keeps LEDs dimmer.`,example:`gpio_set_drive_capability(static_cast<gpio_num_t>(PIN_LED), GPIO_DRIVE_CAP_0);`},{id:`esp-err`,term:`esp_err_t / ESP_OK`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`esp_err_t`,`ESP_OK`,`ESP_FAIL`],calls:[`esp_err_to_name`,`ESP_ERROR_CHECK`]},short:`ESP-IDF functions answer with a result code; <code>ESP_OK</code> (0) means it worked.`,simple:`A receipt: "OK", or a reason why not.`,deep:`Any other value is an error such as <code>ESP_ERR_NO_MEM</code> or <code>ESP_ERR_TIMEOUT</code>; <code>esp_err_to_name()</code> turns it into text for the log. The labs check <code>!= ESP_OK</code> and bail out instead of crashing.`,example:`if (i2s_new_channel(&cc, &tx_, nullptr) != ESP_OK) return false;`},{id:`preferences`,term:`Preferences`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Preferences`],calls:[`prefs.begin`,`prefs.end`,`prefs_.begin`,`prefs_.end`,`p.begin`,`p.end`,`isKey`]},short:`Saves small settings in the chip's flash so they survive power-off and re-flashing.`,simple:`A notebook glued inside the board: write "Wi-Fi name = home" and it is still there tomorrow.`,deep:`Arduino's wrapper around ESP-IDF NVS, a key-value store in its own flash partition. <code>begin("ns", readOnly)</code> opens a namespace; namespaces and keys are at most 15 characters. Not encrypted by default; call <code>end()</code> when done.`,example:`Preferences prefs;`},{id:`preferences-put-get`,term:`putString() / getString()...`,topic:`esp`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`putUChar`,`getUChar`,`putBool`,`getBool`,`putString`,`getString`,`putBytes`,`getBytes`,`getBytesLength`,`putUInt`,`getUInt`,`putInt`,`getInt`,`putULong`,`getULong`,`putFloat`,`getFloat`]},short:`Write a value under a key, or read it back with a fallback if it was never saved.`,simple:`Labelled jars on a shelf: put something in the jar called "vol", and later take it out again.`,deep:`The type is in the name (<code>UChar</code> = <code>uint8_t</code>, <code>UInt</code> = <code>uint32_t</code>). <code>getX(key, fallback)</code> returns the fallback when the key is missing. Writes cost flash wear, so the labs save only on change.`,example:`const uint8_t format = p.getUChar("format", 0);`},{id:`x-task-create`,term:`xTaskCreatePinnedToCore()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`xTaskCreatePinnedToCore`,`xTaskCreate`]},short:`Starts a new task: a function that runs alongside <code>loop()</code>, on a core you choose.`,simple:`Hiring a second cook. They have their own recipe and work at the same time as you, on their own stove.`,deep:`Arguments: function, name, stack size, a <code>void *</code> parameter (often <code>this</code>), priority, an optional handle, core. In ESP-IDF the stack size is in <b>bytes</b>. <code>loop()</code> runs on core 1; the labs pin their readers and samplers to core 0. Returns <code>pdPASS</code> on success.`,example:`xTaskCreatePinnedToCore(task, "door", 12 * 1024, this, 2, nullptr, 0);`},{id:`v-task-delay`,term:`vTaskDelay()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`vTaskDelay`,`vTaskDelayUntil`,`xTaskDelayUntil`,`taskYIELD`]},short:`Puts this task to sleep for a number of ticks, so other tasks get the CPU.`,simple:`The second cook stepping back from the stove for a moment so someone else can use it.`,deep:`Ticks are 1 ms on Arduino-ESP32, but write <code>pdMS_TO_TICKS(ms)</code> so the meaning is clear. A task's endless loop must block or delay somewhere, or lower-priority tasks starve and the watchdog complains.`,example:`vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(80));`},{id:`pd-ms-to-ticks`,term:`pdMS_TO_TICKS()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`pdMS_TO_TICKS`],words:[`portTICK_PERIOD_MS`,`TickType_t`]},short:`Converts milliseconds into FreeRTOS ticks, the unit every wait in FreeRTOS uses.`,simple:`Translating minutes into the kitchen clock's own units.`,deep:`A macro: <code>ms * configTICK_RATE_HZ / 1000</code>. With the 1000 Hz tick here it is one to one, but the code stays right if the tick rate changes. Waits shorter than a tick round down to 0.`,example:`xQueueReceive(mailbox_, &r, pdMS_TO_TICKS(80))`},{id:`port-max-delay`,term:`portMAX_DELAY`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`portMAX_DELAY`]},short:`"Wait forever": pass it as a timeout to block until something arrives.`,simple:`Sitting by the letterbox until the post comes, however long it takes.`,deep:`The largest tick count, which FreeRTOS treats as no timeout. Good for a worker task that has nothing else to do; never use it in <code>loop()</code> or an interrupt.`,example:`i2s_channel_write(tx_, out, sizeof(out), &done, portMAX_DELAY);`},{id:`pd-true`,term:`pdTRUE / pdPASS`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`pdTRUE`,`pdFALSE`,`pdPASS`,`pdFAIL`,`errQUEUE_FULL`]},short:`FreeRTOS's own yes/no answers: <code>pdTRUE</code>/<code>pdPASS</code> mean it worked.`,simple:`The receipt FreeRTOS hands back after a queue or task request.`,deep:`Plain integers (<code>BaseType_t</code>), 1 for true/pass and 0 for false/fail. Compare against them explicitly: <code>xQueueSend(...) == pdTRUE</code>.`,example:`return xQueueSend(mailbox_, &r, 0) == pdTRUE ? r.id : 0;`},{id:`x-queue-create`,term:`xQueueCreate()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`xQueueCreate`,`vQueueDelete`],words:[`QueueHandle_t`]},short:`Makes a queue: a safe mailbox for passing items from one task to another.`,simple:`A post box with a fixed number of slots: one task drops letters in, another takes them out, and they never collide.`,deep:`<code>xQueueCreate(length, itemSize)</code> returns a <code>QueueHandle_t</code>, or null if out of memory. Items are <b>copied</b> in and out, so send small structs, not pointers to things that might disappear.`,example:`mailbox_ = xQueueCreate(6, sizeof(Request));`},{id:`x-queue-send`,term:`xQueueSend()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`xQueueSend`,`xQueueSendToBack`,`xQueueSendToFront`,`xQueueSendFromISR`,`xQueueOverwrite`]},short:`Puts a copy of an item into a queue, waiting up to a timeout if it is full.`,simple:`Dropping a letter in the post box. If the box is full you can wait a moment, or walk away.`,deep:`A timeout of 0 never blocks and returns <code>errQUEUE_FULL</code> when there's no room: right for <code>loop()</code> and web handlers, which must not stall. From an interrupt use the <code>FromISR</code> version.`,example:`if (xQueueSend(queue_, &c, 0) != pdTRUE) log_w("queue full");`},{id:`x-queue-receive`,term:`xQueueReceive()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`xQueueReceive`,`xQueuePeek`,`uxQueueMessagesWaiting`]},short:`Takes the next item out of a queue, waiting up to a timeout if it is empty.`,simple:`Opening the post box: take the oldest letter, or wait a bit for one to arrive.`,deep:`Items come out in order, copied into your variable. A worker task typically blocks here with a timeout (or <code>portMAX_DELAY</code>), using no CPU until work arrives.`,example:`if (xQueueReceive(mailbox_, &r, pdMS_TO_TICKS(80)) == pdTRUE) {`},{id:`semaphore`,term:`xSemaphoreTake() / Give`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`xSemaphoreCreateBinary`,`xSemaphoreCreateMutex`,`xSemaphoreTake`,`xSemaphoreGive`,`xSemaphoreGiveFromISR`],words:[`SemaphoreHandle_t`]},short:`A signal one task waits for and another (or an interrupt) gives.`,simple:`A baton in a relay race: the runner waits until the baton is handed over, then goes.`,deep:`The MFRC522 driver sleeps in <code>xSemaphoreTake(done_, timeout)</code> until the reader's IRQ interrupt calls <code>xSemaphoreGiveFromISR</code>, so no SPI polling is needed. A mutex semaphore adds priority inheritance for locking.`,example:`const bool woke = xSemaphoreTake(done_, pdMS_TO_TICKS(5)) == pdTRUE;`},{id:`task-handle`,term:`TaskHandle_t / vTaskDelete()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`TaskHandle_t`],calls:[`vTaskDelete`,`xTaskGetCurrentTaskHandle`,`xPortGetCoreID`,`uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark`]},short:`A handle names a running task; <code>vTaskDelete</code> ends one.`,simple:`The second cook's name badge, and the note that sends them home.`,deep:`<code>vTaskDelete(nullptr)</code> ends the calling task; a task function must never just return. <code>uxTaskGetStackHighWaterMark</code> shows how close a task came to overflowing its stack.`,example:`vTaskDelete(nullptr);`},{id:`critical-section`,term:`portENTER_CRITICAL()`,topic:`rtos`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`portENTER_CRITICAL`,`portEXIT_CRITICAL`,`portENTER_CRITICAL_ISR`,`portEXIT_CRITICAL_ISR`,`taskENTER_CRITICAL`,`taskEXIT_CRITICAL`],words:[`portMUX_TYPE`,`portMUX_INITIALIZER_UNLOCKED`]},short:`Locks a few lines so no other task, core or interrupt can touch the same data meanwhile.`,simple:`Holding the door shut with your foot for a second while you swap two things on the shelf.`,deep:`Takes a <code>portMUX_TYPE</code> spinlock and disables interrupts on this core, so it works across both ESP32 cores and with interrupts. Keep the section to a few copies: no printing, no waiting, no allocation.`,example:`portENTER_CRITICAL(&lock_);
const uint32_t h = head_;
portEXIT_CRITICAL(&lock_);`},{id:`adafruit-st7789`,term:`Adafruit_ST7789`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Adafruit_ST7789`,`Adafruit_SPITFT`]},short:`The driver for the 1.9-inch 320×170 colour screen used from lesson 03.`,simple:`The interpreter between the board and the screen: you say "draw this", it speaks the screen's language over SPI.`,deep:`Adafruit's ST7789 driver. <code>init(170, 320)</code> sets the panel size, <code>setRotation(1)</code> turns it landscape, <code>setSPISpeed</code> pushes the clock up to 40 MHz. Pixels are RGB565.`,example:`tft_.init(170, 320);`},{id:`adafruit-gfx`,term:`Adafruit_GFX`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Adafruit_GFX`,`GFXfont`]},short:`The drawing library behind every screen call: lines, circles, rectangles, text.`,simple:`A box of stencils and pens. Any screen that can colour one pixel gets all the other shapes for free.`,deep:`An abstract base class: a subclass only has to implement <code>drawPixel</code>, and everything else is built on it (our <code>FrameBuffer</code> also overrides the line and fill calls for speed). Coordinates are <code>int16_t</code>, from the top-left corner.`,example:`class FrameBuffer : public Adafruit_GFX {`},{id:`gfx-canvas16`,term:`GFXcanvas16`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`GFXcanvas16`,`GFXcanvas1`,`GFXcanvas8`]},short:`An off-screen picture in memory that you draw on, then send to the screen in one go.`,simple:`Sketching on paper first and then holding the finished page up to the window, so nobody sees half a drawing.`,deep:`Adafruit GFX's 16-bit canvas: a <code>uint16_t</code> per pixel, 320×170×2 = about 106 KB. It allocates with plain <code>malloc</code>, which here lands in PSRAM; our <code>FrameBuffer</code> prefers internal RAM instead.`,example:`GFXcanvas16 *frame = nullptr;  // 320 x 170 x 2 bytes = 106 KB, lives in PSRAM.`},{id:`frame-buffer`,term:`FrameBuffer`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`FrameBuffer`]},short:`This project's off-screen picture: draw into it with the normal GFX calls, then push it to the screen.`,simple:`Our own sketch pad, kept in the fastest drawer that has room.`,deep:`Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/frame_buffer.h</code>. <code>begin()</code> tries fast internal RAM first and falls back to PSRAM, and <code>internal()</code> says which it got. It overrides <code>drawPixel</code>, <code>fillScreen</code> and the line and rectangle calls with direct writes into <code>pixels()</code>.`,example:`FrameBuffer &fb = display.fb();`},{id:`display`,term:`Display`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Display`],calls:[`setBacklight`]},short:`This project's screen helper: the ST7789, its backlight and the frame buffer, set up the same way in every lab.`,simple:`The screen and its sketch pad packaged together: draw on <code>fb()</code>, then <code>push()</code> shows it.`,deep:`Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/display.h</code>. <code>begin()</code> starts PWM on the backlight, SPI at 40 MHz and the panel in landscape; <code>push()</code> sends the whole picture with one <code>drawRGBBitmap</code>.`,example:`if (!display.begin()) log_e("no memory for the frame buffer");`},{id:`button`,term:`Button`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Button`]},short:`This project's push-button helper: turns raw presses into clean clicks, double-clicks and long presses.`,simple:`A doorman for the button: it ignores the jitter of the metal contacts and tells you what the person really meant.`,deep:`Defined in <code>kit/lib/bench/src/button.h</code>. An <code>IRAM_ATTR</code> interrupt counts every edge; <code>poll()</code> accepts a new level after 25 ms of quiet and reports <code>Event::Click</code>, <code>DoubleClick</code> (within 280 ms) or <code>LongPress</code> (600 ms).`,example:`Button external(PIN_BUTTON, "button");`},{id:`lab-shell`,term:`Lab / BleLab / SoundLab`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Lab`,`BleLab`,`SoundLab`,`LabSpec`,`BleLabSpec`,`SoundLabSpec`],calls:[`lab.begin`,`lab.loop`,`lab.warn`,`lab.note`,`lab.showView`,`lab.claimLed`,`lab.submit`]},short:`The shared shell each track's labs are built on: screen, button, LED, serial commands and hooks.`,simple:`A ready-built workbench. Each lab only brings its own tools (views and commands) and plugs them in.`,deep:`A lab is a <code>...Spec</code>: its views (draw functions plus a double-click action), its serial commands and hooks such as <code>setup</code>, <code>tick</code> and <code>gesture</code>. <code>lab.warn</code> and <code>lab.note</code> print printf-style messages to the log and the screen. Each <code>src/&lt;lab&gt;/main.cpp</code> is mostly that list, so <code>help</code> shows exactly what the lesson teaches.`,example:`BleLab lab(kSpec);
void setup() { lab.begin(); }`},{id:`mfrc522`,term:`Mfrc522`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`Mfrc522`,`MFRC522`,`RC522`]},short:`This project's own driver for the RC522 card reader, written register by register with no library.`,simple:`Speaking to the reader chip directly, one byte at a time, so you can see every word of the conversation.`,deep:`Defined in <code>kit/lib/rfid-kit/src/mfrc522.h</code>: SPI framing, registers, FIFO, the chip's timer and either polling or sleeping on its IRQ wire. <code>iso14443a.h</code> and <code>mifare_classic.h</code> build the card protocol on top.`,example:`const uint8_t v = chip_.begin(*spi_, pins, nullptr);`},{id:`rgb565`,term:`rgb() / hsv() / color565()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`rgb`,`hsv`,`color565`]},short:`Turn red, green and blue (or a hue on the colour wheel) into one 16-bit screen colour.`,simple:`Mixing paint, then writing the mix as a single code the screen understands.`,deep:`RGB565 keeps 5 bits of red, 6 of green and 5 of blue: <code>((r &amp; 0xF8) &lt;&lt; 8) | ((g &amp; 0xFC) &lt;&lt; 3) | (b &gt;&gt; 3)</code>. Our <code>rgb()</code> is <code>constexpr</code>, so palette colours cost nothing at runtime.`,example:`constexpr uint16_t teal = rgb(70, 220, 190);`},{id:`gfx-fill-screen`,term:`fillScreen()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`fillScreen`]},short:`Paints the whole screen (or canvas) one colour.`,simple:`A fresh coat of paint over everything, usually the first step of drawing a new frame.`,deep:`On our <code>FrameBuffer</code> it's a single <code>std::fill_n</code> over 54 400 pixels; on the panel itself it streams that many pixels over SPI.`,example:`fb.fillScreen(pal::night);`},{id:`gfx-draw-pixel`,term:`drawPixel()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`drawPixel`,`writePixel`]},short:`Colours one single dot at x, y.`,simple:`One dot of a felt-tip pen.`,deep:`The one function every Adafruit GFX surface must provide; all other shapes can be made from it. Ours checks the bounds and writes <code>buf_[y * _width + x]</code>.`,example:`fb.drawPixel(x, y, pal::ink);`},{id:`gfx-rect`,term:`fillRect() / drawRect()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`fillRect`,`drawRect`,`fillRoundRect`,`drawRoundRect`]},short:`Draw a rectangle from its top-left corner, width and height: filled, outlined, or with rounded corners.`,simple:`Stamping a box shape onto the picture.`,deep:`Arguments: <code>(x, y, w, h, colour)</code>, plus a corner radius for the round versions. <code>fill</code> paints the inside, <code>draw</code> only the outline. Bars, panels and buttons on every lab screen are made of these.`,example:`fb.fillRoundRect(x, y, 140, 24, 6, pal::dim);`},{id:`gfx-circle`,term:`fillCircle() / drawCircle()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`fillCircle`,`drawCircle`,`fillEllipse`,`drawEllipse`,`fillTriangle`,`drawTriangle`,`drawCircleHelper`,`fillCircleHelper`]},short:`Draw round shapes and triangles: circles from a centre and radius, triangles from three corners.`,simple:`A compass and a set square for the screen.`,deep:`<code>fillCircle(cx, cy, r, colour)</code> uses the centre, not a corner. Triangles take <code>(x0, y0, x1, y1, x2, y2, colour)</code>. All built from fast horizontal lines on our frame buffer.`,example:`fb.fillCircle(cx, cy - ry - s / 2, max(2, s / 7), c.accent);`},{id:`gfx-line`,term:`drawLine() / drawFastHLine()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`drawLine`,`drawFastHLine`,`drawFastVLine`,`writeLine`,`writeFastHLine`,`writeFastVLine`]},short:`Draw a straight line between two points, or a fast horizontal or vertical one.`,simple:`A ruler and pencil. The "fast" ones only go straight across or straight down, and are much quicker.`,deep:`<code>drawLine(x0, y0, x1, y1, c)</code> uses Bresenham's algorithm, one pixel at a time. <code>drawFastHLine(x, y, w, c)</code> fills a run of pixels with one <code>std::fill_n</code> on our frame buffer, so dividers and bars use it.`,example:`fb.drawFastHLine(0, 22, 320, pal::dim);`},{id:`gfx-cursor`,term:`setCursor() / print()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`setCursor`,`getCursorX`,`getCursorY`,`setTextWrap`,`fb.print`,`fb.printf`,`tft.print`]},short:`<code>setCursor</code> sets where text starts; <code>fb.print</code> then writes text there on the screen.`,simple:`Putting the pen down at a spot before writing.`,deep:`With the built-in font the point is the text's top-left; with a custom <code>GFXfont</code> it is the left end of the baseline, so y must be lower. <code>print</code> and <code>printf</code> (same <code>%</code> codes as <code>Serial.printf</code>) move the cursor along as they write.`,example:`fb.setCursor(8, 16);`},{id:`gfx-text-style`,term:`setTextColor() / setFont()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`setTextColor`,`setTextSize`,`setFont`],words:[`FreeSans9pt7b`,`FreeSansBold9pt7b`,`FreeSansBold12pt7b`,`FreeSansBold18pt7b`,`FreeSansBold24pt7b`]},short:`Choose the colour, size and font of the text you print next.`,simple:`Picking which pen and which lettering stencil to use.`,deep:`<code>setTextColor(fg)</code> draws transparent text; <code>setTextColor(fg, bg)</code> also paints the background (built-in font only). <code>setFont(&amp;FreeSans9pt7b)</code> picks a smooth font that ships with Adafruit GFX (9 pt, regular); <code>setFont(nullptr)</code> returns to the 6×8 built-in font; <code>setTextSize(2)</code> doubles it.`,example:`fb.setTextColor(pal::muted);`},{id:`gfx-text-bounds`,term:`getTextBounds()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`getTextBounds`]},short:`Measures how wide and tall some text would be, without drawing it.`,simple:`Measuring a sign before hanging it, so you can centre it on the wall.`,deep:`<code>getTextBounds(text, x, y, &amp;x1, &amp;y1, &amp;w, &amp;h)</code> fills in the box the text would cover with the current font and size; used to centre and right-align labels.`,example:`fb.getTextBounds(s, 0, 0, &x1, &y1, &w, &h);`},{id:`gfx-rgb-bitmap`,term:`drawRGBBitmap()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`drawRGBBitmap`]},short:`Copies a whole block of pixels onto the screen in one go.`,simple:`Sliding a finished photo into the frame, instead of painting it dot by dot.`,deep:`<code>drawRGBBitmap(x, y, pixels, w, h)</code> streams RGB565 data over SPI; a full 320×170 frame is about 106 KB, roughly 22 ms at 40 MHz. That is the frame rate's upper limit, which is why the labs draw off-screen first.`,example:`tft_.drawRGBBitmap(0, 0, fb_.pixels(), kW, kH);`},{id:`gfx-rotation`,term:`setRotation()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`setRotation`,`invertDisplay`,`setSPISpeed`]},short:`Turns the screen's picture in steps of 90°, so it reads the right way up.`,simple:`Turning a photo frame from portrait to landscape.`,deep:`0-3 are 0°, 90°, 180°, 270°. Rotation 1 makes the 170×320 panel a 320×170 landscape screen; width and height swap accordingly.`,example:`tft_.setRotation(1);`},{id:`wifi-begin`,term:`WiFi.begin()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`WiFi.begin`,`WiFi.disconnect`,`WiFi.isConnected`,`WiFi.status`,`WiFi.setAutoReconnect`],words:[`WL_CONNECTED`]},short:`Starts joining a Wi-Fi network with its name and password.`,simple:`Knocking on the router's door with the password; the answer comes a few seconds later.`,deep:`Returns at once; joining happens in the background Wi-Fi task. Our <code>Connection</code> class listens for the events (connected, got IP, disconnected) instead of waiting in a loop. Passing <code>nullptr</code> as the password joins an open network.`,example:`WiFi.begin(ssid_.c_str(), pass_.isEmpty() ? nullptr : pass_.c_str());`},{id:`wifi-mode`,term:`WiFi.mode()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`WiFi.mode`,`WiFi.getMode`,`WiFi.softAP`,`WiFi.softAPIP`,`WiFi.softAPdisconnect`],words:[`WIFI_STA`,`WIFI_AP`,`WIFI_AP_STA`,`WIFI_OFF`]},short:`Chooses whether the board joins a network (station), makes its own (access point), or both.`,simple:`Being a guest at someone else's party, hosting your own, or doing both at once.`,deep:`<code>WIFI_AP_STA</code> runs both on one radio, so they share a channel; the setup portal uses it to show a page while it tries your network. <code>softAP</code> starts the board's own network, reachable at <code>192.168.4.1</code> by default.`,example:`WiFi.mode(WIFI_AP_STA);`},{id:`wifi-info`,term:`WiFi.localIP() / WiFi.RSSI()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`WiFi.localIP`,`WiFi.RSSI`,`WiFi.SSID`,`WiFi.channel`,`WiFi.macAddress`,`WiFi.gatewayIP`,`WiFi.BSSIDstr`],words:[`IPAddress`]},short:`Ask the Wi-Fi connection for details: the board's IP address, signal strength, channel, MAC.`,simple:`Reading the board's house number on the network, and how many bars of signal it has.`,deep:`<code>localIP()</code> is an <code>IPAddress</code> (<code>toString()</code> for text). <code>RSSI()</code> is in dBm: about -50 is excellent, below -80 is weak. All are 0 or empty until connected.`,example:`log_i("IP %s, %d dBm", WiFi.localIP().toString().c_str(), WiFi.RSSI());`},{id:`wifi-scan`,term:`WiFi.scanNetworks()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`WiFi.scanNetworks`,`WiFi.scanComplete`,`WiFi.scanDelete`,`WiFi.encryptionType`]},short:`Listens for nearby Wi-Fi networks and lists their names, signal and security.`,simple:`Turning the radio dial to hear which stations are around.`,deep:`With <code>async = true</code> it returns at once (-1, still running); poll <code>scanComplete()</code> for the count, read each entry, then <code>scanDelete()</code> to free the list. A scan takes a few seconds across all channels.`,example:`const int16_t r = WiFi.scanNetworks(/*async=*/true, /*show_hidden=*/true);`},{id:`wifi-events`,term:`WiFi.onEvent()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`WiFi.onEvent`],words:[`WiFiEvent_t`,`WiFiEventInfo_t`,`ARDUINO_EVENT_WIFI_STA_GOT_IP`,`ARDUINO_EVENT_WIFI_STA_DISCONNECTED`]},short:`Registers a function the Wi-Fi driver calls when something happens: connected, got an IP, dropped off.`,simple:`Asking the receptionist to ring you when your visitor arrives, instead of waiting in the lobby.`,deep:`The callback runs in the Wi-Fi event task, not in <code>loop()</code>, so it should only set flags; <code>loop()</code> does the real work. <code>info</code> carries details such as the disconnect reason.`,example:`WiFi.onEvent([this](WiFiEvent_t event, WiFiEventInfo_t info) { onEvent(event, info); });`},{id:`mdns`,term:`MDNS.begin()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`MDNS.begin`,`MDNS.addService`,`MDNS.end`]},short:`Gives the board a friendly name like <code>bench.local</code> on your network.`,simple:`A name tag, so other devices can find the board without knowing its number.`,deep:`Multicast DNS answers name queries on the local network only; macOS and iOS support it out of the box. <code>addService("http", "tcp", 80)</code> also advertises the web server.`,example:`if (!mdnsUp_ && MDNS.begin(kHostname)) {`},{id:`ntp-time`,term:`configTzTime() / getLocalTime()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`configTzTime`,`configTime`,`getLocalTime`]},short:`Sets the clock from internet time servers, then reads the local date and time.`,simple:`The board has no battery-backed clock, so it asks the internet "what time is it?" once it's online.`,deep:`<code>configTzTime(tz, server1, server2)</code> sets the POSIX time zone and starts SNTP. <code>getLocalTime(&amp;tm, timeoutMs)</code> fills a <code>struct tm</code> and returns <code>false</code> until the first sync has happened.`,example:`const int hour = getLocalTime(&lt, 0) ? lt.tm_hour : 0;`},{id:`http-client`,term:`HTTPClient`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`HTTPClient`,`NetworkClientSecure`,`WiFiClientSecure`,`WiFiClient`],calls:[`http.begin`,`http.end`,`http.GET`,`http.POST`,`http.sendRequest`,`http.getString`,`http.addHeader`,`http.setTimeout`]},short:`Makes web requests from the board, like a tiny browser: ask a URL, get the answer back.`,simple:`Sending a letter to a website and waiting for its reply.`,deep:`Arduino-ESP32's HTTP client over a TCP or TLS connection. Our <code>https::get</code> checks the server's certificate against the bundled Mozilla roots. It blocks for 0.3 s to several seconds, so call it from a worker task, never from <code>loop()</code>.`,example:`HTTPClient http;`},{id:`json-document`,term:`JsonDocument`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`JsonDocument`,`JsonObject`,`JsonObjectConst`,`JsonArray`,`JsonArrayConst`,`JsonVariant`,`JsonVariantConst`]},short:`ArduinoJson's container for JSON data: fill it like a dictionary, or read one you received.`,simple:`A filing tray with labelled folders: <code>doc["heap"] = 1234</code> puts a number in the folder called heap.`,deep:`ArduinoJson 7: a <code>JsonDocument</code> grows on the heap as needed. <code>doc["k"].to&lt;JsonArray&gt;()</code> makes nested arrays; <code>doc["k"] | fallback</code> reads with a default if the key is missing or the wrong type.`,example:`JsonDocument doc;
doc["heapFree"] = ESP.getFreeHeap();`},{id:`serialize-json`,term:`serializeJson()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`serializeJson`,`serializeJsonPretty`,`measureJson`]},short:`Turns a <code>JsonDocument</code> into JSON text, ready to send.`,simple:`Typing up the filing tray's contents into one tidy letter.`,deep:`<code>serializeJson(doc, out)</code> writes into a <code>String</code>, a <code>char</code> buffer or a stream such as <code>Serial</code>, and returns the number of bytes written.`,example:`serializeJson(doc, out);`},{id:`deserialize-json`,term:`deserializeJson()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`deserializeJson`],words:[`DeserializationError`,`DeserializationOption`]},short:`Reads JSON text into a <code>JsonDocument</code> you can look things up in.`,simple:`Opening a letter and sorting its contents into the labelled folders.`,deep:`Returns a <code>DeserializationError</code> that is true when something went wrong (<code>e.c_str()</code> says what). A <code>Filter</code> keeps only the fields you need, which saves a lot of RAM on big weather replies.`,example:`if (DeserializationError e = deserializeJson(doc, r.body)) {`},{id:`async-web-server`,term:`AsyncWebServer`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`AsyncWebServer`,`HTTP_GET`,`HTTP_POST`,`HTTP_PUT`,`HTTP_DELETE`,`ArRequestHandlerFunction`],calls:[`AsyncURIMatcher::exact`,`server_.on`,`server_.begin`,`server_.addHandler`]},short:`A web server on the board: your phone or laptop opens a page or API, and the board answers.`,simple:`A tiny shop window: each address (<code>/api/card</code>) has a clerk who knows what to reply.`,deep:`ESPAsyncWebServer answers in its own network task, so the screen and the reader never wait. <code>server_.on(path, HTTP_GET, handler)</code> registers a route; handlers must be quick and must not block, so heavy work is queued to another task.`,example:`server_.on(AsyncURIMatcher::exact("/api"), HTTP_GET, [](AsyncWebServerRequest *req) { ... });`},{id:`async-request`,term:`AsyncWebServerRequest`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`AsyncWebServerRequest`,`AsyncWebParameter`,`AsyncResponseStream`],calls:[`req.send`,`req.hasParam`,`req.getParam`,`req.arg`,`request.send`]},short:`One incoming web request: what was asked for, and how to answer it.`,simple:`The customer at the shop window: you read their order and hand back a reply.`,deep:`Handlers receive it as a pointer, hence <code>req-&gt;send(200, "application/json", body)</code>. <code>hasParam</code>/<code>getParam</code> read query or form fields. Every request must get exactly one <code>send</code>.`,example:`req->send(200, "application/json", kApiIndex);`},{id:`async-web-socket`,term:`AsyncWebSocket`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`AsyncWebSocket`,`AsyncWebSocketClient`,`AwsEventType`,`WS_EVT_CONNECT`,`WS_EVT_DISCONNECT`,`WS_EVT_DATA`],calls:[`textAll`,`ws_.count`,`ws_.cleanupClients`]},short:`A live two-way line to the browser, so the board can push updates the moment something happens.`,simple:`A phone call instead of letters: the line stays open and either side can speak any time.`,deep:`Mounted on the web server at a path such as <code>/ws</code>. <code>textAll</code> sends to every connected browser; <code>onEvent</code> hears connects, disconnects and messages. Call <code>cleanupClients()</code> now and then to drop connections that went away.`,example:`if (ws_.count()) ws_.textAll(wrap("status", json));`},{id:`nimble-device`,term:`NimBLEDevice`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEDevice`],calls:[`NimBLEDevice::init`,`NimBLEDevice::setPower`,`NimBLEDevice::getPower`,`NimBLEDevice::setMTU`,`NimBLEDevice::getAddress`]},short:`The front door to Bluetooth LE: starts the radio stack and hands out servers, scanners and clients.`,simple:`The Bluetooth reception desk: say hello once with <code>init</code>, then ask it for whatever you need.`,deep:`NimBLE-Arduino, a lighter BLE stack than Bluedroid. Its functions are <code>static</code>, so they are called as <code>NimBLEDevice::...</code> with no object. <code>init(name)</code> sets the advertised name; <code>setPower</code> sets transmit power in dBm.`,example:`if (!NimBLEDevice::init(spec_.deviceName)) {`},{id:`nimble-server`,term:`NimBLEServer / NimBLECharacteristic`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEServer`,`NimBLEService`,`NimBLECharacteristic`,`NimBLEDescriptor`,`NIMBLE_PROPERTY`,`NimBLEAttValue`],calls:[`NimBLEDevice::createServer`,`NimBLEDevice::getServer`,`createService`,`createCharacteristic`]},short:`The board's own Bluetooth menu: services group characteristics, small values a phone can read, write or subscribe to.`,simple:`A noticeboard with labelled slots: a phone walks up, reads the temperature slot, or pins a message in the message slot.`,deep:`GATT: a service (a UUID) holds characteristics, each with a UUID, properties such as <code>READ | WRITE | NOTIFY</code> and a value up to the MTU. <code>notify()</code> pushes a new value to subscribed phones without being asked.`,example:`chr_[Led] = svc->createCharacteristic(uuid(1), NIMBLE_PROPERTY::READ | NIMBLE_PROPERTY::WRITE, 3);`},{id:`nimble-advertising`,term:`NimBLEAdvertising`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEAdvertising`,`NimBLEAdvertisementData`],calls:[`NimBLEDevice::getAdvertising`,`startAdvertising`]},short:`Makes the board shout "I'm here!" a few times a second, so phones can see and connect to it.`,simple:`A lighthouse: short flashes at regular intervals that anyone nearby can spot.`,deep:`A legacy advertising packet carries at most 31 bytes (name, flags, service UUIDs, manufacturer data), plus 31 more in a scan response.`,example:`NimBLEAdvertising *adv = NimBLEDevice::getAdvertising();`},{id:`nimble-scan`,term:`NimBLEScan`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEScan`,`NimBLEScanResults`,`NimBLEAdvertisedDevice`,`NimBLEScanCallbacks`,`NimBLEAddress`],calls:[`NimBLEDevice::getScan`]},short:`Listens for other Bluetooth devices advertising nearby and reports each one found.`,simple:`Watching the coastline for lighthouse flashes and writing down each one you see.`,deep:`Each <code>NimBLEAdvertisedDevice</code> has an address, RSSI and payload. Active scanning also asks for scan responses. Callbacks run in the NimBLE host task, so hand results to <code>loop()</code> rather than drawing there.`,example:`NimBLEScan *scan = NimBLEDevice::getScan();`},{id:`nimble-client`,term:`NimBLEClient`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEClient`,`NimBLERemoteService`,`NimBLERemoteCharacteristic`,`NimBLERemoteDescriptor`],calls:[`NimBLEDevice::createClient`]},short:`The board as the visitor: connects to another Bluetooth device and reads or writes its values.`,simple:`Walking up to someone else's noticeboard instead of hosting your own.`,deep:`The central role. Connect, discover services (<code>getServices(true)</code>), then read, write or subscribe to remote characteristics. Connecting and discovery block, so B8 does them in a worker task.`,example:`const std::vector<NimBLERemoteService *> &services = client_->getServices(true);`},{id:`nimble-conn-info`,term:`NimBLEConnInfo`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEConnInfo`]},short:`Details of one Bluetooth connection: who's connected, how it's secured, how big a packet can be.`,simple:`The visitor's badge: name, whether they showed ID, and how much they can carry at once.`,deep:`Passed to callbacks such as <code>onConnect</code> and <code>onAuthenticationComplete</code>: the peer's address, connection handle, MTU, and whether the link is encrypted, authenticated and bonded.`,example:`void onConnect(NimBLEServer *s, NimBLEConnInfo &c) override {`},{id:`nimble-callbacks`,term:`NimBLE...Callbacks`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{words:[`NimBLEServerCallbacks`,`NimBLECharacteristicCallbacks`,`NimBLEClientCallbacks`,`NimBLEDescriptorCallbacks`]},short:`Classes you inherit from to hear about Bluetooth events: connects, disconnects, writes, reads.`,simple:`Leaving your phone number with the reception desk: "call me when someone arrives".`,deep:`Override only the <code>virtual</code> functions you need (<code>onConnect</code>, <code>onWrite</code>...), then register the object with <code>setCallbacks</code>. They run in the NimBLE host task, so keep them short and thread-safe.`,example:`class ClientLink : public NimBLEClientCallbacks {`},{id:`nimble-security`,term:`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityAuth()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityAuth`,`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityIOCap`,`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityInitKey`,`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityRespKey`,`NimBLEDevice::startSecurity`,`NimBLEDevice::injectPassKey`,`NimBLEDevice::injectConfirmPasskey`,`NimBLEDevice::getNumBonds`,`NimBLEDevice::getBondedAddress`,`NimBLEDevice::deleteBond`,`NimBLEDevice::deleteAllBonds`]},short:`Decides how a Bluetooth link is secured: pairing, passkeys and remembered bonds.`,simple:`The door policy: let anyone in, ask for a code, or only let in friends you've met before.`,deep:`<code>setSecurityAuth(bond, mitm, sc)</code> asks for bonding, man-in-the-middle protection and LE Secure Connections; the IO capability decides whether a passkey or a yes/no comparison is shown. Bonds are stored in NVS and survive restarts.`,example:`NimBLEDevice::setSecurityAuth(true, true, true);`},{id:`ble-note`,term:`BLE_NOTE()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`BLE_NOTE`]},short:`This project's shortcut for adding a line to the Bluetooth labs' activity log.`,simple:`A quick jot in the lab notebook that the screen and serial log both show.`,deep:`A macro from <code>kit/lib/ble-kit/src/ble_link.h</code> that expands to <code>::ble::activity().add(::ble::Act::kind, ...)</code>: a printf-style entry in a ring buffer guarded by a critical section, so callbacks in the NimBLE task can write safely.`,example:`BLE_NOTE(Info, "Vault set (%u bytes)", arg.length());`},{id:`i2s-channel`,term:`i2s_new_channel()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`i2s_new_channel`,`i2s_channel_init_std_mode`,`i2s_channel_enable`,`i2s_channel_disable`,`i2s_del_channel`],words:[`i2s_chan_handle_t`,`i2s_chan_config_t`,`i2s_std_config_t`]},short:`Sets up an I2S sound channel to a digital microphone or speaker amplifier.`,simple:`Laying a conveyor belt for sound: a clock keeps the beat, and one number rides along for every sample.`,deep:`ESP-IDF 5's I2S driver: <code>i2s_new_channel</code> creates TX and/or RX channels, <code>i2s_channel_init_std_mode</code> sets the sample rate, bit width and pins (standard Philips I2S format), <code>i2s_channel_enable</code> starts the clocks and DMA.`,example:`if (i2s_new_channel(&cc, &tx_, nullptr) != ESP_OK) return false;`},{id:`i2s-read-write`,term:`i2s_channel_read() / write()`,topic:`libraries`,kind:`api`,match:{calls:[`i2s_channel_read`,`i2s_channel_write`]},short:`Takes samples from the microphone, or hands samples to the speaker, through the I2S channel.`,simple:`Picking parcels off the conveyor belt, or putting them on it.`,deep:`<code>i2s_channel_read(handle, buf, bytes, &amp;got, timeoutMs)</code> copies from the DMA buffers, waiting if none are ready. Writes wait while the DMA is full, so a speaker task runs exactly as fast as the sound plays.`,example:`i2s_channel_write(tx_, out, sizeof(out), &done, portMAX_DELAY);`}],t={basics:{title:`Building blocks`,blurb:`The small symbols that do the sums, comparisons and choices in every line.`},types:{title:`Kinds of values`,blurb:`Whole numbers, decimals, text and yes/no: what each box can hold.`},flow:{title:`Making decisions & repeating`,blurb:`How the code chooses a path, loops, and finishes a job.`},functions:{title:`Functions`,blurb:`Named recipes the code can run, from setup() and loop() to tiny lambdas.`},memory:{title:`Memory & const`,blurb:`Where values live, how to point at them, and how to keep them from changing.`},classes:{title:`Types you build`,blurb:`Structs, classes, enums and namespaces: your own kinds of things.`},modern:{title:`Modern C++`,blurb:`Compile-time constants, templates, casts and the standard library's helpers.`},preprocessor:{title:`Before compiling`,blurb:`Lines starting with # that rearrange the code before the compiler reads it.`},arduino:{title:`Arduino basics`,blurb:`Pins, timing, serial printing and text: the everyday Arduino toolkit.`},esp:{title:`The ESP32 chip`,blurb:`Logging, memory, timers and storage that are special to this chip.`},rtos:{title:`Doing two things at once`,blurb:`FreeRTOS tasks, queues and locks that let the board multitask safely.`},libraries:{title:`Libraries`,blurb:`The screen, Wi-Fi, web server, JSON, Bluetooth and sound helpers the labs use.`}};export{e as GLOSSARY,t as TOPIC_LABELS};
